    ],
    "title": "**Inserting a list item always spawns a fresh run-leader**",
    "text": "**Inserting a list item always spawns a fresh run-leader**: see [Open Issues](../issues/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE32GRZG002) — live-tested while writing this same Resolved section. A freshly `aperas insert`-ed bullet always parses as its own orphaned one-item list, so it gets its own explicit `orderedList`/`startIndex` per the normalization rule — marking it a run-leader relative to whatever precedes it, even an ordinary continuation of the identical list. Renders with a spurious blank line before it; not corruption (bullet type and numbering stay correct), but a real regression from the old anchor-owns-it model, where a freshly inserted item carried no prop of its own. Confirmed live twice more since: pushing a list's full corrected content instead of a bare `--after` insert avoids it, since the new item joins the same parsed run as a continuation rather than becoming its own orphaned list. **Resolved**: `kg:insert` (`kgInsert.ts`) now looks at the sibling landing immediately before the new item — for a bare `--after`, that sibling itself; for `--before`/append, whatever's already adjacent — and if it's a plain `listItem` carrying no `orderedList`/`startIndex` of its own (an ordinary continuation), strips the freshly-parsed item's own copy of those props before writing, so it silently joins that run instead of starting a spurious new one. Scoped deliberately to that one confirmed-live shape — landing right before an existing run's own leader is a different case (the new item would need to *take over* the leader role, not just avoid claiming one) and isn't handled.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE32GXH000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
