        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**",
    "text": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**: reproduced twice in `list-consumption`'s eval sandbox — a new bullet's content lands on a stale, already-tombstoned id instead of a fresh one, and `tombstonedAt` is never cleared, so the content stays invisible even though the summary line reports a clean match/add. **Resolved**: root cause was every old-side matching pool (Stage A's leaves and heading buckets, Stage B's containers) including tombstoned candidates with no exclusion at all — a live new node's content could bind to a dead id purely because its stripped text happened to match. Fixed by excluding every tombstoned old child from every matching pool in `diffChildren`: a dead slot is never reused, new content always gets a fresh id instead. Verified live: a new item with text identical to an already-tombstoned sibling's now lands on a brand-new id, not the dead one. Same fix as [item 1 above](#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005:links:Link:00CGRWEKXR000",
        "@type": "Link",
