    "title": "## Implementation Plan",
    "text": "Batched, not fixed by the original filing order below — that plan was sketched before `reconcile.ts` was actually read, and wasn't followed as written once it was. [Open Issues](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002) are grouped by which batch actually closes them, per [Task Breakdown](#id/BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Task Breakdown",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G004",
      "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G005",
      "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G006",
      "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G007",
      "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G008",
      "BlockNode:00CGJWKA0G001",
      "BlockNode:00CGK2C16G001",
      "BlockNode:00CGK3AH60001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T07:19:45.584Z",
    "title": "**Investigate, read-only — done**",
    "text": "**Investigate, read-only — done**: read `packages/core/src/reconcile.ts` in full. Root cause for [items 1](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003) and [2](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004) is Stage B's per-type container alignment (`diffChildren`), which zips `listItem` siblings by raw array position with zero content comparison — not Stage A's `matchKeyed` as first suspected, since `listItem` is a `CONTAINER_TYPE`, never reaching Stage A at all. [Item 3](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005) and [item 4](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG006) do **not** share this fix: item 3 is `matchLeftoverByAbstract`, used only for artifact/folder rename detection, never for list-item matching; item 4 is `pruneUnreachableTombstones` never marking a fully-dead container itself dead, an unrelated GC gap. Both left open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T07:19:47.063Z",
    "title": "**Fix Group A — done for items 1 and 2, items 3 and 4 correctly out of scope**",
    "text": "**Fix Group A — done for items 1 and 2, items 3 and 4 correctly out of scope**: [items 1](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003) and [2](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004) fixed together — Stage B's `listItem` buckets now key-matched through the same `matchKeyed`+`leafKey` machinery Stage A already uses for leaves, plus every tombstoned old candidate excluded from every matching pool (Stage A and B both), so live content can never bind to a dead id. Verified against the exact reproducible scenarios already on record, and against a fresh direct reproduction (a 4-item list, item 2 tombstoned-but-present, piped without it) plus an ordinary reorder/insert-in-the-middle case that turned out to be silently broken the same way. [Item 3](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005) and [item 4](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG006) remain open — see the corrected finding above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T07:19:48.397Z",
    "title": "**Fix item 6 — done**",
    "text": "**Fix item 6 — done**: [multi-artifact `kg:ingest` isn't transactional](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG008), fixed by isolating each file's ingest in its own try/catch (`ingestOne`) rather than the validate-everything-first approach originally proposed — a true all-or-nothing batch would need a store-wide snapshot/rollback primitive that doesn't exist here, a bigger undertaking than this item's scope, so failures are isolated and reported per file instead, matching the pattern this module already used for `untracked`. `retryDanglingRefs`, called at the end of every `kg:ingest` run, had the identical unguarded loop and got the same fix, though it was never named by the issue. Verified live: a batch with one file failing on a genuine full-slug-path collision still ingested the other file and reported the failure rather than aborting before the folder-tree rebuild.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T07:19:49.457Z",
    "title": "**Item 7 was not deferred — the \"no design exists\" premise didn't survive reading the actual restriction**",
    "text": "**Item 7 was not deferred — the \"no design exists\" premise didn't survive reading the actual restriction**: [the Folder/Artifact holder-promotion gap](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG009) turned out narrower than filed. `ArtifactNode extends BlockNode` and already passes `kg:update`'s own type check — piping any content through `kg:update` on a holder `ArtifactNode` already cleared `.holder`, no design or code change needed there. The real, narrower gap was `FolderNode` alone, and the fix was mechanical, not a design question: extend `kg:insert`'s already-existing anchor-*less* bare-promote branch (the same one a `BlockNode` holder already uses) to also accept a `FolderNode` target, rejecting a `FolderNode` + `--after`/`--before` combination outright since a folder's position comes from its own path, never list order. No new promotion design was needed because the promotion mechanism (`hydrateFromParsed`'s existing `this.holder = undefined`) already existed and was already correct — the only thing missing was a caller that could reach it graph-first. Verified live on a scratch holder chain (`resolve --create-holder`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T07:19:50.525Z",
    "title": "00CGJWJY88001",
    "text": "Move each fixed item from `issues/core.md`'s Open Issues to Resolved as it lands, with the real root cause recorded — not just \"fixed.\" **Done** for every item actually fixed in this pass (items 1, 2, 6, 7, and the unplanned props-only item below); items 3 and 4 correctly stayed in Open Issues, since neither was touched.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Verification Plan",
    "text": "A fix isn't done until its Open Issues entry can honestly move to Resolved with the actual mechanism named, matching this concern's own citation-direction discipline against guessing. Batch 1 verified live against direct reproductions built for each mechanism (a tombstone-interleaved list push, a same-batch multi-file ingest failure, a scratch holder-promotion chain), every scratch test run against the real service and cleaned up immediately after — twice, a mistake mid-verification was itself caught and recovered via the staged `git` checkpoint before continuing. Batch 2 gets the same bar once it's investigated: a reproduction on record before any diff, not a fresh test written to fit whatever the fix turns out to be.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF23FB1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF23SXQ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
