      "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR001",
      "BlockNode:00CF5N94NR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Philosophy — the maturation principle",
    "text": "**That substance matures; it does not merely accumulate.** [current] Both axes run the same direction — Apeiron toward Peras, unbounded toward bounded. On the **content** axis, prose hardens from formless discussion into typed design, issues, planning, history. On the **topology** axis, a relationship first exists *indirectly*, mediated by a discussion node that cites both ends and carries the judgment in its own text; once it proves load-bearing it crystallizes into a *direct* citation, and the mediating node's job is done. Link density is the residue of that lifecycle, which is why it reads as maturity from outside rather than as tidiness — and why `discussion` is where the cost of it gets paid.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Orientation — link maturity",
    "text": "**A link is placed at the maturity the relationship has earned.** [current] Writing means placing links, but not all of them direct and not all at once. A relationship that still needs judgment to state goes into a mediating discussion node citing both ends; one that has proven load-bearing becomes a direct citation between them. Reaching for a direct link too early asserts a dependency nobody has tested; leaving one mediated forever makes every traversal pay for the hop.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR004:links:Link:00CGRTWWG0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF46W4QR003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR004:links:Link:00CGRTWWG0000:props:StringProp:00CGRTWWG0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Discipline — edit loop step 5, rewritten",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v1's step 5](#id/BlockNode:00CF46W4QR003)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR005",
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR005:props:StringProp:00CF4QNYG8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR005:props:StringProp:00CF4QNYG8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "5"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CF4QNWZR005",
    "text": "**Deep write — follow what the change made stale, and link what was never linked.** Check backlinks and forward links; update what now disagrees — and add the citation where the change has just made a relationship real. Closing an issue that a design or a fix resolves means linking the two to each other before moving on, in both directions, not only the one that happened to get written first. This is the per-edit instance of dense linking, and the only moment it is cheap: you are already standing where the missing link is visible.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF4QNX00000",
    "text": "**Project, then stage.** `aperas project <path> --flush`, then `git add` immediately.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00001:links:Link:00CGRTXH80000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF46W4QR00F",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00001:links:Link:00CGRTXH80000:props:StringProp:00CGRTXH80001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Discipline — Promotion links at the origin, closing sentence regrounded",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v1's closing sentence](#id/BlockNode:00CF46W4QR00F)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF5MTTSG002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Discipline — The dense-linking pass",
    "text": "[current] Occasionally, and deliberately, work the relationships instead of the content. Bring distant nodes into one view, then take each pair in turn and ask whether a real, nameable dependency exists. If one does and a plain citation carries it, link directly, respecting direction. If stating it takes judgment, write a mediating discussion node that cites both ends and holds the reasoning in its own text.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00003",
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The counterweight matters as much as the practice",
    "text": "The counterweight matters as much as the practice: **do not manufacture links.** Two docs sharing a corpus, or sharing vocabulary, is not a dependency. The test is whether you can say in one sentence what one owes the other. A pass that adds twenty weak links has made the graph harder to traverse, not denser — deep read now descends into noise.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF4QNX00004",
    "text": "Run it when a concern set has grown without anyone standing back from it, when two concerns keep coming up together, or when one finding turns out to have been recorded in several places independently. That last case is itself the evidence: the relationship existed and nothing captured it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Discipline — A view is per-task, not per-session",
    "text": "[current] Orientation's rule is that a view left pointing at last session's work is worse than no view. The practice that follows: re-unfold as the task's scope moves, and treat any view you did not open yourself as unknown until checked. `aperas tree --view <name>` renders whatever was unfolded whenever, with nothing marking age, so an inherited view looks identical to one built for the question actually in front of you.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF4QNX00006",
    "text": "Cheapest discipline is a named view per task rather than one long-lived default — creating one is a single call, and a view scoped to the task documents its own contents. The cleanup gap underneath this is real and tracked: nothing prunes a view's `unfolds` against what is still live (`issues/treeview.md`), so stale refs accumulate silently in any view kept across tasks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00005",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Mechanics — Comparing distant docs: the view as a lens",
    "text": "[current] `--view` is not a bookmark list; it is the mechanism for putting nodes that sit far apart in the tree next to each other. Unfold every doc being compared into one view, then render it once:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00008",
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00009",
      "BlockNode:00CF4QNX0000A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF4QNX00008",
    "text": "```bash\nfor f in issues/a.md discussion/b.md design/c.md; do\n  aperas unfold \"$f\" --view <name> --flush\ndone\naperas tree --view <name>\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00007",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF4QNX00009",
    "text": "Run `aperas backlinks <id> --text` on a target before adding a link to it — working across several docs at once makes it easy to add a citation that already exists. And note that a link resolves by its `id/` fragment: the leading relative path is for the human reader, so a stale path still resolves correctly while misleading anyone who reads it. Observed live renaming a concern — every fragment kept working, every path string had to be fixed by hand.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00007",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX0000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-13T01:19:50.218Z",
    "title": "00CF4QNX0000A",
    "text": "---",
