          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**v1.1 — the vertical thread between levels was repaired, and a structural audit found one of the gaps itself**",
    "text": "**v1.1 — the vertical thread between levels was repaired, and a structural audit found one of the gaps itself**: crystallization is now stated at Philosophy and Orientation rather than existing only as a Discipline rule grounded off-level in the design doc, and dense linking is realized below Orientation rather than only asserted there — including the restoration of a concrete trigger v1 had silently dropped from v0's item 18. Seven additions, recorded verbatim as [a delta on the v1 snapshot](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001) rather than as a fresh snapshot, since a snapshot is taken at a major version and minor versions thread onto it. One addition was not found by reading at all: an audit of [the vertical thread](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CF4D9XT0001) queried every principle for realizers below it and returned zero for \"keep an active view,\" a gap nobody had named, now closed by its own Discipline section. The same audit missed the crystallization gap from the backward direction and caught it from the forward one — the realizer's grounding link pointed outside the level structure — which is recorded as the sharper lesson: the query inherited the tooling's asymmetry, since `backlinks` is a verb and forward traversal is not.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Versioning — snapshot, delta, thread",
    "text": "The skill file is a *generative* projection of this concern rather than a mechanical one: no command serializes design into SKILL.md, so the two can drift in a way an `aperas project` artifact cannot. Three devices keep the drift legible, and together they are the meta workflow's maintenance half.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF595QQR002",
      "BlockNode:00CF595QQR003",
      "BlockNode:00CF595QQR004",
      "BlockNode:00CF5N6B88001",
      "BlockNode:00CF5N6B88002",
      "BlockNode:00CVM39ANG001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF595QQR002",
    "text": "A **snapshot** is a verbatim copy of the skill file taken at a major version and split so every item is individually addressable. Copy, not projection: a projection holds only the current version, so what a restructure *drops* would survive nowhere but git archaeology. That loss is not hypothetical — v0's snapshot is the only reason a trigger v1 silently dropped was ever found. Successive snapshots make the skill's evolution the object of study rather than just its latest state.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF595QQR003",
    "text": "A **delta** records a minor version's additions verbatim against the standing snapshot, rather than re-snapshotting the whole document. The base stays stable, what changed stays legible, and each addition is addressable on the same terms as the snapshot's own items.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF595QQR004",
    "text": "A **thread** wires each Philosophy and Orientation principle to the items that realize it below, as mediating nodes — mediating because the snapshot must stay verbatim to stay comparable. The thread is what makes the level structure auditable instead of merely asserted: a principle with no realizer is a measurement, not an impression. Read backward, from a principle's backlinks, it detects a principle stated and never operationalized. Read forward, from a realizer's grounding, it detects the opposite defect — an item whose explanation sits outside the level structure, which is the observable form of a principle that was never stated at all. It cannot detect *under*-realization, since presence and sufficiency are not the same measurement and this graph is too coarse to count the difference; that judgment stays with the reader.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Top matter — Status bumped to v1.1",
    "text": "Status: **v1.1** — v1 restructured a flat, incident-ordered list of eighteen items into four levels, abstract to concrete; the arrangement was the defect. v1.1 repairs the vertical thread between those levels: crystallization stated at Philosophy and Orientation rather than only as a Discipline rule, and dense linking realized below Orientation rather than only asserted there. Concern docs: `AperasKG/artifacts/{design,issues,planning,history,discussion}/aperas-skill.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN854YV0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN854YV0000:props:StringProp:00CN87B9H8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "105"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN854YV0002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CN6SMSX0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN854YV0002:props:StringProp:00CN87B9YR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "184"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN87B94G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF46W4QR00C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002:links:Link:00CN87B94G000:props:StringProp:00CN87B94G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Discipline — Citation direction, exception generalized",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v1's \"one sanctioned exception\" paragraph](#id/BlockNode:00CF46W4QR00C)\nFolded into design: [Citation direction](../design/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00H) and [its incompleteness test](../design/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CN6SMSX0001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR003",
      "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CN853N1G001",
    "text": "The sanctioned exception is a **normative, singular** pointer — one whose target is the block's whole referent rather than one of many possible mentions. A design doc's own `# Context` section is the familiar instance (one link per facet, a fixed set), but it is the *property* that is sanctioned, not that location: a history milestone announcing one snapshot, or a design block naming *the* canonical exemplar, qualify the same way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CN854AT0001",
    "text": "The test is whether removing the link leaves the block **incomplete**. A milestone whose content is \"this snapshot was taken\" no longer says what it exists to say once its pointer is gone; \"here is a discussion that also touched on this\" loses only enrichment, and stays forbidden. What the rule is actually against is a *list of children* — unbounded, discretionary, accumulating — so cardinality is the real criterion and direction is only its usual symptom. [current] Generalized after a milestone needed exactly such a pointer and the rule, stated as \"designated index,\" named an instance where it meant a property.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Mechanics — Checking the skill against its own record",
    "text": "[current] `SKILL.md` is a *generative* projection of its concern — authored from design rather than serialized by a command — so unlike an `aperas project` artifact it can drift from its source with nothing detecting it. `scripts/skill_drift.py` compares the file against the graph's record of it (the latest snapshot plus the deltas above it):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR002",
      "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF5GV8FR002",
    "text": "```bash\nscripts/skill_drift.py              # both directions\nscripts/skill_drift.py --added      # written into the file, never recorded\nscripts/skill_drift.py --dropped    # recorded, no longer in the file\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR001",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF5GV8FR003",
    "text": "Run it after editing this file, before considering the edit done. *Added* is the check that matters most — an edit made and not recorded is invisible from the file's own side, which is how three separate additions in one session went unrecorded until a reader noticed. *Dropped* catches the opposite: v1 silently lost one of v0 item 18's three triggers, and nothing flagged it. Both are heuristics over normalized text, so a reworded sentence inside an otherwise-matching paragraph can still slip past.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5GV8FR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001:props:StringProp:00CF5H3WM8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001:props:StringProp:00CF5H3WM8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CF5H3V20001",
    "text": "**`scripts/skill_drift.py` — drift detection for a generative projection, and it caught an omission on its first real run**: three times in one session an edit was made to `SKILL.md` and never recorded in the graph, each caught only by a reader noticing. Every fix attempted was another prose rule, which is the wrong instrument for a failure of remembering. This is the mechanical version: compare the file against the graph's record of it — the latest snapshot plus the deltas above it — in both directions. *Added* finds text written into the file that was never recorded; *dropped* finds recorded items no longer in the file, which is how v1 silently lost one of v0 item 18's three triggers.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20002",
      "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20003",
      "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20004",
      "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20002:props:StringProp:00CF5H3WM8002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20002:props:StringProp:00CF5H3WM8003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**First real run found a genuine omission**",
    "text": "**First real run found a genuine omission**: the citation-direction generalization — two paragraphs of substantive rule change — had landed before the rest of v1.1 and was recorded in no delta at all, along with the `Status` bump itself. Both were found mechanically, of exactly the class that had needed a human three times running. Now recorded.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Calibration was not free, and the rounds are informative**",
    "text": "**Calibration was not free, and the rounds are informative**: the baseline had to be narrowed to the *latest* snapshot (comparing against v0 reports v1's entire restructure as drops, since a major version is supposed to differ); the subtree walk had to run in reading order, since a stack-based walk scrambles it and breaks any fingerprint spanning a node boundary; list markers had to be normalized away, because the parser lifts them into props so the graph stores `**Philosophy** — …` where the file has `1. **Philosophy** — …`; fences had to be stripped on both sides rather than one; and the granularity had to drop from section to paragraph, since a section-opening fingerprint only catches whole new sections and misses an addition made *inside* an existing one — which was four of this version's seven additions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A real gap in the mechanism this validates**",
    "text": "**A real gap in the mechanism this validates**: the delta format records *additions*, not *supersessions*. When a minor version rewrites an existing item the delta stores the new text and nothing marks which snapshot item it replaced, so those items show under *dropped* and need a human read to distinguish \"deliberately superseded\" from \"silently lost\" — which is the exact distinction the check exists to make. A `supersedes` pointer in the delta entry would close it. Worth noting the shape: the same missing-predicate problem as everywhere else in this corpus, arriving now in the versioning format rather than in the link model.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**It remains a heuristic over normalized text**",
    "text": "**It remains a heuristic over normalized text**: a reworded sentence inside an otherwise-matching paragraph slips past, and commentary written *inside* a delta entry reads as dropped because it is legitimately not in the file. Sits at the *skill script* stage of the tooling path — staged and shared, not yet a first-class verb.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5H3V20001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5MTTSG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF5MTTSG002",
    "text": "This is the topology half of crystallization, stated at Philosophy above: a relationship first mediated by a discussion node becomes a direct citation once it proves load-bearing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNX00001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5N6B88001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF5N6B88001",
    "text": "A delta entry that *replaces* rather than adds carries a **`Supersedes:`** line naming the snapshot item it retires, as an ordinary wikilink. Without it, an item a minor version deliberately rewrote is indistinguishable from one silently dropped — which is the single judgement the whole arrangement exists to support, so leaving it to a reader's memory defeats the purpose. The marker is bookkeeping rather than skill text, and is excluded from the verbatim comparison on both sides.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5N6B88002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CF5N6B88002",
    "text": "Drift between the file and this record is then mechanically checkable, which is the point: `scripts/skill_drift.py` reports text written into the file but never recorded, recorded items absent from the file with no entry claiming to supersede them, and a `Status:` line in any of the four skills whose version differs from the newest recorded one. The first two defects had previously been caught only by a reader noticing, three times in one session for the first and once across a major version for the second. The check is gated, not advisory: the script exits nonzero on any of the three findings, and the chain `skill_drift.py && aperas check-links` that closes a skill-only task stops on drift.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF595QQR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5N94NR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Mechanics — Supersedes marker, added to the drift-check section",
    "text": "A delta entry that replaces rather than adds carries a `Supersedes: [title](#id/BlockNode:...)` line naming the item it retires, which is what lets the check tell a deliberate rewrite from a silent loss.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF4QNWZR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001:props:StringProp:00CF5NDZ4G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001:props:StringProp:00CF5NDZ4G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CF5NDXG8001",
    "text": "**`Supersedes:` folded into the delta format — the check can now tell a rewrite from a loss**: the gap the drift check exposed in its own mechanism, closed by the cheapest thing that could work: a delta entry replacing rather than adding carries a `Supersedes: [title](#id/BlockNode:...)` line naming the item it retires. An ordinary wikilink, parsed by convention, no engine change and no new predicate — the marker is bookkeeping rather than skill text, so it is stripped from the verbatim comparison on both sides, which also stops it reading as dropped content itself. Applied to v1.1's three rewrites (edit-loop step 5, the citation-direction exception, the promotion-links closing sentence); the check now reports zero unaccounted drops and attributes all three to the entries that replaced them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8002",
      "BlockNode:00CF5NDXGG000",
      "BlockNode:00CF5NDXGG001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8002:props:StringProp:00CF5NDZ4G002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8002:props:StringProp:00CF5NDZ4G003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**What it cost to leave the convention out**",
    "text": "**What it cost to leave the convention out**: before the marker, the three legitimately-rewritten items were indistinguishable from silently-lost ones, so every run ended in \"needs a human read\" — which is the judgement the arrangement exists to make, handed straight back to the reader it was supposed to relieve. A check whose output requires the same attention as not having it is not much of a check.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXGG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A verbatim record has to stay verbatim**",
    "text": "**A verbatim record has to stay verbatim**: commentary written *inside* a delta entry (\"caught by the drift check on its first run\") read as dropped content, correctly, because it is not in the file. Fixed by convention rather than by code — a delta entry holds the replacement text and its marker, nothing else; narrative belongs in Freeflow or the thread. The earlier root-intro exclusion was the same lesson arriving one level up.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXGG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Loop closed on itself again**",
    "text": "**Loop closed on itself again**: documenting the marker in the skill's own Mechanics required recording that documentation as a delta entry, which the check then verified. Three rounds of this now — edit, record, verify — and it is starting to feel less like discipline and more like the shape the work has.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF5NDXG8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5ZE1S0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF5ZE1S0001:links:Link:00CGRTZQ70000",
        "@type": "Link",
