          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Top matter — Frontmatter description: reordered imperative-first",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v1.3's first frontmatter, ordered identity-first](#id/BlockNode:00CF8BZWM0002)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF80MKFG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF9SRRFG002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF9SRRFG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "--- name",
    "text": "--- name: aperas description: Before anything else in this project — first turn, every session, whatever the task looks like, including when it looks unrelated, trivial, read-only, or like one quick lookup — check memory for a standing decision on whether Aperas manages this session. Do this without loading this skill, and never ask permission to do it or offer it as an option; just do it. If a decision is recorded, follow it. If none is, ask with AskUserQuestion using exactly these choices — Never / Not now / Yes, this session only / Yes, all sessions — and record only 'all sessions' or 'never' durably, so a session-scoped answer is asked again next session. Load this skill only once the answer is yes; it then governs the work — orient in the graph before acting, change it through the CLI rather than by editing files, put back what the work turns up. Aperas is this project's external memory: a knowledge graph, worked through the `aperas` CLI, holding what has been decided, tried, found and planned. For first-time ingest of existing documents, `kg-doc-ingest` covers that; this one first. ---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF9SRRFG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ1R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ1R001:props:StringProp:00CFA7PRNG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ1R001:props:StringProp:00CFA7PRNG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Nothing in the skill fires on a read**",
    "text": "**Nothing in the skill fires on a read**: orientation is stated only as step 1 of the edit loop, so a task whose whole investigation phase is reading concerns never enters the loop and never reaches a rule at all. Confirmed live: a session that loaded the skill on turn one and consented to it still read the entire task context out of `artifacts/**.md` with `Read`/`find`/`grep`, and made its first `aperas` call only when a plan step mechanically required the CLI — after the code edit had already landed. The fix is a mechanical substitution rule at Orientation: an `artifacts/**.md` path about to become an argument to `Read`/`grep`/`find`/`cat` is a node reference, and `aperas unfold <ref>` is what it meant. **Resolved in v1.4**: Orientation now carries the substitution rule — an `artifacts/**.md` path bound for `Read`/`grep`/`find`/`cat` is a node reference, and `aperas unfold` is what the call meant — stated for reading rather than only for writing, and naming `Apeiron/*.jsonld` and source code as the two things deliberately not over the line.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ20000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CFA7PQ20000",
    "text": "**\"The edit loop\" is a misnomer that causes the failure it should prevent**: the name tells a reader the section applies once they start writing, so a long read-only phase — and any source-code work — reads as outside it. It is the workflow itself, not an edit procedure: the same opening applies whether the task ends in a traversal, a graph change, or a code change, and only the tail differs. Renaming it and stating its entry condition is what makes the read-side and code-side rules reachable instead of bolted on. **Resolved in v1.4**: the section is now *The loop*, with its entry condition stated — reading a concern, touching source the graph covers, or changing the graph all put you in it — one shared opening and a branching tail.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ20001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CFA7PQ20001",
    "text": "**The shadow rule's only worked example is structural, so reading a projection for content reads as exempt**: Philosophy contrasts a `grep` over projections against one `aperas backlinks` call — a question about link structure. Nothing states the content case, and for pure text retrieval the projection genuinely is equivalent, so an agent asking content questions correctly concludes the example doesn't apply to it. The missing corollary is already implied by Philosophy's own sentence that a node's meaning is constituted by what it links to, what links back, and where it sits: a projection carries the text and drops precisely what constitutes the meaning. **Resolved in v1.4**: Philosophy now states the content case directly, that a projection carries a block's words and none of its place, so the loss is invisible precisely because the words are identical.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ20002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CFA7PQ20002",
    "text": "**No entry point for source-code work the graph already carries a concern about, at either end**: a task framed as \"fix this bug in `reconcile.ts`\" matches nothing in the skill, though the graph very likely holds issues, design, planning and history for that exact file. Two obligations are missing rather than one — before the work, find what the graph already knows (a root cause identified, a fix already ordered, an approach already rejected); after it, record what the work found, since a fix landed with nothing written is invisible from every side that can be consulted. **Resolved in v1.4**: Discipline gains *Source the graph already has a concern about*, obliging a lookup before the source is read and a recording after the work lands, with the loop's second trigger pointing at it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFA7PQ20003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`scripts/show_node.py` is documented without noting it reads the on-disk mirror**",
    "text": "**`scripts/show_node.py` is documented without noting it reads the on-disk mirror**: Mechanics presents it as the reader for raw node state, but it parses `Apeiron/*.jsonld` directly, so after an unflushed mutation it reports pre-flush state while `aperas unfold`/`tree` report the live service's. Confirmed live: a move and a tombstone were both invisible to it, producing a confident and wrong conclusion that the move had gone backwards — recovered only by flushing and re-reading. **Resolved in v1.4**: Mechanics now says the script reads the on-disk mirror and reports pre-flush state after an unflushed mutation, where `aperas unfold`/`tree` report the live service's.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
