    ],
    "title": "**v1.3 — the trigger gap was closed, and closing it revealed that invocation is not governance**",
    "text": "**v1.3 — the trigger gap was closed, and closing it revealed that invocation is not governance**: two earlier sessions had investigated `reconcile.ts`/`node.ts` by plain file reads with no traversal and no skill load at all — the frontmatter description named only graph-editing triggers, so a task framed as source-code work never matched it. A mechanical `PreToolUse` gate built to close that broke three independent ways under live hook tracing and was retired for being personal, machine-specific and invisible to anyone who had not set it up; v1.3 traded it for a one-time consent question the skill asks and remembers, and widened its scope from graph edits to the whole session. Recorded as [the v1.3 delta](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CF80MKFG001). The description that shipped still did not fire, and what fixed it was pure ordering — the identity sentence demoted beneath an unconditional imperative keyed on session position, amended onto the same commit and [recorded as its own delta entry](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CF9SRRFG001) once `skill_drift.py` reported it written-but-unrecorded. Notable for what it exposed rather than what it fixed: with the skill loading on turn one, a session still read an entire task out of the projections and edited code before its first `aperas` call, which is what [Batch 2 of Planning](../planning/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CFAMC7XR000) now exists to answer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001:links:Link:00CGRSZ3C0000",
