    "title": "**GFM task-list marker leak traced to its exact upstream mechanism — and it's about the first inline node's type, not about bold specifically**",
    "text": "**GFM task-list marker leak traced to its exact upstream mechanism — and it's about the first inline node's type, not about bold specifically**: [the round-trip issue](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638007) was closed as \"does not reproduce\" twice, both times using plain-text task items. Confirmed live pushing GFM checkboxes onto this session's own discussion plan (six bold-lead-in items, e.g. `- [x] **1. Title**: rest`): every one came back with the marker baked into `.text` as literal prose (`[x] **1. Title**: rest`), the `checked` prop notwithstanding. Reproduced in isolation via `parseMarkdownTree`, then traced past `astParser.ts` entirely — it has no marker-stripping logic of its own, only `setProp('checked', ...)` off mdast's own `node.checked` — into `node_modules/mdast-util-gfm-task-list-item/lib/index.js`'s `exitParagraphWithTaskListItem`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1DDF58003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CGJHDK68002",
      "BlockNode:00CGJHDK6G000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGJHDK68002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CGJHDK68002",
    "text": "The tokenizer for `[x] `/`[ ] ` consumes only the bracketed part as its own token; the one mandatory space after it is left as ordinary paragraph content. `exitParagraphWithTaskListItem` exists specifically to trim that leftover character and re-base the paragraph's own `position.start` past it — but only here: `const head = node.children[0]; if (head && head.type === 'text') { head.value = head.value.slice(1); ...; node.position.start = Object.assign({}, head.position.start) }`. When the paragraph's first inline child genuinely is a `text` node, that leading space survives inline tokenization as the first character of `head.value`, the slice works, and the position moves forward with it — which is what `astParser.ts`'s `rawSlice` then trusts. When the first child is `strong`, `emphasis`, `inlineCode`, or `link` instead, CommonMark's inline tokenizer never materializes a lone leading space with nothing else before an inline span as its own text node at all — it's absorbed into inter-token whitespace and disappears from the tree. So `head.type === 'text'` is false, the whole trim-and-rebase block never runs, and the paragraph's `position.start` is left pointing at the space right after `- `, one character before where real content starts. `rawSlice` then faithfully includes the leftover `[x] `/`[ ] ` as literal source text, because from the position's own point of view, that is where the paragraph begins.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGJHDK68001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGJHDK6G000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CGJHDK6G000",
    "text": "Verified live across every non-text leading inline type, not only bold — `*italic*`, `` `code` ``, and `[link](url)` all leak identically; only a plain-text-first item is ever cleaned. The real trigger condition is \"does this task item's content start with plain text,\" narrower than \"bold\" and broader than the original report implied.",
