    "title": "**(1) Historical staleness has no standing safeguard, only a one-time hand sweep**",
    "text": "**(1) Historical staleness has no standing safeguard, only a one-time hand sweep**: `resolveBlockLinks` used to run only from `kg:ingest`, so any node last touched before that entry point existed (or before a later fix) carries a permanently-empty `.links` no matter what its text cites — a graph-wide sweep found and fixed 9 real cases (see [History](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003)), but the sweep was a one-time hand pass, not a standing safeguard. A fresh corpus-wide check today found the same symptom already back on nodes the sweep never touched (e.g. `planning/core.md`'s own Batch 1 item 1, `BlockNode:00CGGTQ620002` — untouched since the sweep, `.links` empty despite an unchanged real citation in its text). The sweep fixed a snapshot; nothing keeps re-checking it, so drift just accumulates again under a different name. Needs a repeatable sweep, not another one-time hand pass.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ38WX0G001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJV8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**(2) A fresh regression, mechanism unknown**",
    "text": "**(2) A fresh regression, mechanism unknown**: four nodes (`BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005`, `BlockNode:00CE1GW638004`, `BlockNode:00CE1GW638006`, `BlockNode:00CE8T6W7G001`) each had a link genuinely resolve — `aperas update --text-only` reported \"N resolved\" for every one of them — and each still ended up with an empty `.links` afterward, discovered only because a reader manually re-checked one by direct id. Extensive attempts to reproduce it under controlled conditions all failed to trigger the loss: matching move-then-`--text-only`-update sequences (single node and 4-node batches), a sibling heading's plain reconcile in between, a full `aperas service stop`/`start` cycle, and the exact stop → manual `ArtifactNode.jsonld` edit → start sequence used elsewhere in the same session. Re-running each affected node's own unchanged `update --text-only` a second time did resolve and this time verifiably persist the link, so the fix for the *symptom* is cheap once found — the *cause* is not. Needs a reproduction before any fix can be attempted at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000:links:Link:00CJ3C21N0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000:links:Link:00CJ3C21N0000:props:StringProp:00CJ3C21N0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "505"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Link Integrity Checking built (`aperas check-links`)**",
    "text": "**Link Integrity Checking built (`aperas check-links`)**: resolved — the drift-style check has been built (`checkLinkIntegrity` and `repairLinkIntegrity` in `@aperas/core`), and its first landed version had a real gap, since fixed. It scans for live nodes where internal link codes exist in `node.text` but have no corresponding resolved `Link` in `.links`, and repairs them via the `--repair` flag, catching both historical staleness and transient regressions without needing to know the root cause (see [Open Issues](#id/BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001)). **Found on review, fixed**: the original match logic compared each text occurrence against `.links` by raw substring containment against the target's opaque id — reading as thorough (it correctly found and fixed 40 real historical discrepancies across 7 artifacts on its first live run) but actually sound only for codes that textually embed the id (`path#id/<ID>`, `aperas://id/<ID>`), which happens to be every code this corpus currently writes. It could not have verified `[[<deep-path>]]` addressing (`resolve.ts`'s deep-path grammar — a real, currently-live convention, \"used extensively\" in `Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`'s own self-references per `history/linking.md`) at all: a heading-path string shares no substring with the opaque id it resolves to, so a perfectly-resolved deep-path link would have been reported as missing. The one test meant to catch a genuine drop (deleting a `Link`'s `predicate` quad) also turned out to be vacuous — the match logic never reads `predicate`, and the scratch fixture already carried unrelated discrepancies of its own, so the assertion could never actually fail regardless of whether the deletion did anything (confirmed live: the count was identical before and after). **Actually fixed**: `checkLinkIntegrity` now resolves each occurrence for real via `resolveOneCode` (`artifacts.ts`, the same dispatch `resolveBlockLinks` itself uses, exported and called with `createHolder: false` so a scan can never mutate the graph as a side effect), and only reports a code that resolves to a live target with no matching entry in `.links` — an unresolved code is left alone, since that's routine (a bare `#fragment` anchor) or already `danglingRef`/`retryDanglingRefs`'s own separate concern. `verify.ts`'s step 19 replaced the vacuous predicate-quad deletion with a precise before/after discrepancy-count assertion against a real, freshly-resolved link.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
