    "title": "**`flush --clobber` / `reload --discard` round-trip mechanics, startup GC, and shutdown logging design**",
    "text": "**`flush --clobber` / `reload --discard` round-trip mechanics, startup GC, and shutdown logging design**: investigated `reload --discard` and `flush --clobber` interaction, live, at the user's own request after a `flush --clobber` unexpectedly removed a real backlog. Mismatches observed were traced to two *unrelated* mechanisms conflated at first: mark-and-sweep tombstone GC (`pruneUnreachableTombstones`, legitimate — the backlog really was dead) and a duplicate-`@id` collision in `Profile.jsonld` (a real, separate data bug, wrongly described here as \"RDF deduplication\" as if it were a working, intentional mechanism — corrected in [issues/core.md](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CHXSZ56R001), which now carries its own entry). Discovered that prior service restarts left GC garbage on disk because `shutdown()`'s guarded `flushIfDirty()` silently refused and dropped a computed GC result whenever disk had diverged externally, while service startup never ran the sweep at all. First design pass: (1) run the sweep at startup, (2) reword the shutdown warnings, (3) return GC metrics from `flush --clobber`. Reviewed afterward, on request, for correctness and completeness against the actual code rather than the summary of what was intended — found both (1) and (2) didn't actually work: (1) computed the sweep but never set `dirty`/`stateDirty` (declared *after* that block ran in the original patch), so nothing was ever flushed; (2) and every other service-side message, including (1)'s own new log line, went to `console.error`, which is unreachable in real operation since the service is always spawned with `stdio: 'ignore'` (`serviceClient.ts`). Actually fixed: reordered the startup sequence so the sweep can flush immediately when it finds something, and added a durable `logService()` helper (append to a `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`-based log file) used everywhere `console.error` alone used to be the only channel. Also closed a gap the first pass didn't touch at all: `kg:reload` never surfaced its own GC counts, only `kg:flush --clobber` did — `reloadStore()` now returns the same shape and `kg:reload` prints it the same way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGRQKZQR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-16T03:41:22.024Z",
    "title": "### Standing link integrity checking script (`checkLinkIntegrity` / `aperas check-links`)",
    "text": "**Standing link integrity checking script (`checkLinkIntegrity` / `aperas check-links`)**: As specified in [issues/linking.md](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000), building a drift-style check script `checkLinkIntegrity` (and CLI verb `aperas check-links`) compares live block text against `.links` in Oxigraph Store. Scans for live nodes where internal link codes (`[[code]]`, `aperas://...`, `#id/BlockNode:...`) exist in `node.text` but have no corresponding resolved `Link` in `.links`. Supports `--repair` flag to auto-re-resolve and flush missing links, catching both historical drift and transient regressions without needing to know the root cause. Executed live sweep on corpus (`aperas check-links --repair`), detecting 40 historical link integrity discrepancies across 7 artifacts, re-resolving them all, and verifiably restoring the graph to 0 discrepancies.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMX88R0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
