  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYJEXVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Discipline — the end-of-batch sweep, and the two checks that need nothing from you",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CHYJ6PR8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CHYJHB5G002",
      "BlockNode:00CHYJHB5G003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYJHB5G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CHYJHB5G002",
    "text": "**At the end of a batch — not after each step — run `aperas check-links` once before handing back.** The per-write check the service runs on its own only sweeps the artifact that was written; this is the pass that catches the damage that lands *elsewhere* — an edit in artifact A breaking a citation that lives in artifact B, which nothing scoped to A can see. It costs about 0.6s against the whole corpus, so the reason to run it once per batch rather than per edit is noise, not expense.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CHYJEXVR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYJHB5G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CHYJHB5G003",
    "text": "The other two link checks need nothing from you, and that is the point — the two recorded losses were both found by a human happening to look, never by a check that fired. The service now sweeps the written artifact after every `update`/`insert`/`remove` and reports anything that write resolved but failed to persist, right under the write's own `Links: N resolved…` line; and it sweeps the whole corpus at startup and on `reload`, carrying any finding on *every* later response until it clears. When either one speaks up, it is describing a link that already looks fine everywhere else — treat it as real and re-run the write (which has fixed it before) or `aperas check-links --repair`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CHYJEXVR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001:props:StringProp:00CHYM5398000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001:props:StringProp:00CHYM5398001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`check-links --repair` re-mints every `Link` id in an affected artifact, not just the broken one**",
    "text": "**`check-links --repair` re-mints every `Link` id in an affected artifact, not just the broken one**: found while verifying the standing sweep end to end — one deliberately-dropped link edge, repaired, produced a 63-insertion/37-deletion diff across `BlockNode.jsonld`, because `repairLinkIntegrity` re-ran `resolveBlockLinks` over *every* block carrying link text in the affected artifact, and that regenerated each `Link` subdocument (and its `position` `StringProp`) under a fresh id even where nothing was wrong. Targets and positions were preserved, so this was churn rather than data loss — but `Link` ids are addressable in their own right: a `TreeView.unfolds` entry can name one (`node.ts`'s `unfold(ref)` accepts a `Link` id), so a repair could silently orphan an unfolded link, which `pruneStaleUnfolds` then cleans up on the next sweep — the viewer's own fold state quietly lost to a repair that touched an unrelated block. Same identity-preservation principle the skill applies to blocks, one tier down.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ0W09C8000",
      "BlockNode:00CJ0XSZ8R001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYN1NH8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYN1NH8001:links:Link:00CHYN1S3G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYN1NH8001:links:Link:00CHYN1S3G000:props:StringProp:00CHYN1S3R000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "863"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CHYN1NH8001",
    "text": "**Built and verified live, same session.** The scoped post-write check and the corpus boundary check both landed as designed; the end-of-batch sweep is recorded in the skill (v2.5). Verified end to end rather than by reading the summary line: one real link edge dropped from `BlockNode.jsonld` on disk, service restarted → the startup sweep named the offending block, that warning then rode unrelated `tree` and `path` calls (proving the \"emerges unbidden\" property the design turned on), and `--repair` cleared it. Two things the build itself turned up, neither anticipated: the corpus sweep was first placed *before* `markReady`, where it eats into `waitForReady`'s 5s budget — moved after, and queued, since nothing needs it complete before the first request; and `--repair` re-mints every `Link` id in the artifact it touches rather than only the broken one, [filed separately](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYNCTRG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYNCTRG001:links:Link:00CHYNCZ1R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CGMX88R0003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYNCTRG001:links:Link:00CHYNCZ1R000:props:StringProp:00CHYNCZ1R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1500"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYNCTRG001:links:Link:00CHYNCZ1R002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CHYNCTRG001:links:Link:00CHYNCZ1R002:props:StringProp:00CHYNCZ20000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "2174"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Three layers of link checking, only one of which anyone has to remember — agreed, built and verified live**",
    "text": "**Three layers of link checking, only one of which anyone has to remember — agreed, built and verified live**: the check existed but nothing triggered it, which is the same failure this concern keeps hitting (\"being asked is the part that fails\"). Measured before designing, since the design turned on it: a full-corpus sweep is ~0.6s of service-side work (1428 live blocks, 358 carrying link text, CLI startup subtracted over three runs), so \"too heavy to run often\" — the premise the first cut rested on — was simply false. Built: **(1)** a scoped, service-side check after every `update`/`insert`/`remove`, sweeping only the written artifact and diffed against a sweep taken just before the write, so it reports only what *that* write broke, on that op's own response directly under its `Links: N resolved…` line — the exact line that lied in both recorded incidents, now self-verifying; `ingest` is excluded deliberately, having always run `resolveBlockLinks` correctly. **(2)** a corpus-wide check at startup and on `reload`, held in a `linkWarning` attached to *every* response the way `contentConflict` already is, so drift that landed while the service was down emerges on the very next command of any kind. **(3)** a corpus-wide sweep at end of batch, the one step left to discipline (skill v2.5, folded into *Stage each verified step*), because it alone catches an edit in artifact A breaking a citation that lives in artifact B. All three report and never repair: the known workaround for [the unreproduced regression](#id/BlockNode:00CGMX88R0003) is \"re-run the identical write and it sticks,\" and a silent auto-retry would erase the evidence it is still being hunted with. Verified end to end rather than from a summary line: one real link edge dropped on disk, service restarted → startup sweep named the block, the warning then rode unrelated `tree` and `path` calls, and `--repair` cleared it. Two things the build itself turned up: the corpus sweep was first placed *before* `markReady`, eating into `waitForReady`'s 5s budget (moved after it, and queued), and `--repair` re-mints every `Link` id in the artifact it touches rather than only the broken one, [filed separately](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMX88R0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ01QAJ0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ01QAJ0000:props:StringProp:00CJ01QE88000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ01QAJ0000:props:StringProp:00CJ01QE88001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CJ01QAJ0000",
    "text": "**`aperas tree` had no way to name where a render actually started, or where a viewcone zoom moved it to** — every line only ever carried an id and a title, so reading a `tree`/`unfold` render meant a separate `aperas path <id>` call to place it in the corpus at all. First attempt over-corrected: a `(path)` tag on *every* line, immediately caught as wrong — repeating the full path on each descendant says the same prefix over and over, since every line under a render's own apex is already relative to it by construction. **Resolved**: exactly two breadcrumb points instead, each printed once, in `aperas://tree/<path>` form (`aperas path`'s own format, directly reusable as a `<ref>`). (1) The apex `aperas tree <ref>` was actually pointed at, as the render's own first line, computed in `kgTree.ts` rather than inside `TreeNode.renderTree()` itself, so `verify.ts`'s own direct `renderTree()` calls keep their existing line-count assumptions untouched. (2) In `--view` mode, wherever a link escapes its owner's viewcone into its own nested one (§13 — the target's own structural position loses canonical placement to the link, an \"escaping win\"), that position, indented to match — an ordinary in-cone link or a superseded pointer line changes nothing about what \"here\" means, so neither gets one. Verified live: constructed an actual escaping-link case (unfold a link whose target's own natural position is folded elsewhere) and confirmed the zoom breadcrumb appears exactly there, nowhere else.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW63800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001:props:StringProp:00CJ02061R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001:props:StringProp:00CJ02061R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CJ0QD2BR001",
    "text": "**`npm run verify` costs ~5 minutes, dominated by a process-lifetime slowdown neither store-freshness nor heap size explains** — instrumented per-step (a `console.log`-wrapping timer keyed on each step's own `\"N. Testing...\"` header, printing a sorted breakdown even on failure) and found the same operation (`checkLinkIntegrity`'s corpus sweep, and separately `parseMarkdownTree`-driven ingestion in Linking Slice steps 13-18) costs low hundreds of ms freshly isolated, but 12-55 *seconds* run as `verify.ts`'s own later steps — steps 3-6 doing the identical kind of parsing work stay fast, steps 7-12 in between do no heavy parsing at all, then 13 onward is uniformly slow. Two credible causes ruled out by direct experiment, not by inspection: (1) oxigraph's own WASM memory footprint (`external`, ~850MB-1GB by that point vs ~50MB fresh) looked causal, but compacting the store mid-run (dehydrate a scratch dir, rehydrate a brand-new `Store` with the identical live content) made the same sweep *slower*, not faster — ruling out \"this one store instance is bloated.\" (2) V8 heap-size tuning (`--max-old-space-size=8192 --max-semi-space-size=128`) changed nothing measurably.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ0QG048001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ023M08001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CJ023M08001",
    "text": "**`npm run verify`'s runtime had no visibility and two real, fixable costs** — nobody could say which of its ~30 steps made a run slow, and two genuine inefficiencies existed unnoticed alongside whatever the process-lifetime slowdown turns out to be (see the still-open entry above). **Resolved, three parts**: (1) a permanent, zero-cost-when-quiet step timer (wraps `console.log`, keys on each step's own numbered header, prints a sorted breakdown at the end even on failure via an outer `try`/`finally`) — the diagnostic that made the still-open finding above possible to state precisely at all. (2) `astParser.ts` built a fresh `unified().use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)` pipeline on every single call across three call sites, two of them (`collectLinkCodesFromText`, `findLeadInSpliceOffset`) hit once per block rather than once per document — hoisted into two module-level singletons (one with `remarkFrontmatter` for whole-document parsing, one without, since running the frontmatter plugin over an arbitrary block's own text risks misreading a stylistic `---` thematic break as a frontmatter delimiter). Measured to cost nothing on its own (isolated timing was identical before and after) but removed as real, confirmed waste regardless. (3) `verify.ts`'s own step 19 called `checkLinkIntegrity` immediately before `repairLinkIntegrity`, which recomputed the identical report internally as its own `reportBefore` — `repairLinkIntegrity` now takes an optional pre-computed report, cutting one of four full corpus sweeps in that step. Net effect on the actual numbers: step 19 dropped ~40s, full suite ~326s -> ~290s — real, but small next to the ~230s the still-open process-lifetime slowdown accounts for across steps 13-19.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ02BTG0001",
