    ],
    "title": "**Practical problem resolved, root cause still not**",
    "text": "**Practical problem resolved, root cause still not**: since the leading hypothesis was V8-engine-level degradation intrinsic to one long process (only a genuinely fresh process/V8 isolate resets it), split `verify.ts` into two files instead of chasing the exact mechanism further — `verify.ts` (steps 1-12, `npm run verify`) and the new `verifyExtended.ts` (steps 13-19, `npm run verify:slow`), sharing only small structural helpers (`verifySupport.ts`). Measured live: `verify` now runs in ~42s (down from ~290-326s); `verify:slow` alone runs the same steps 13-19 in ~70-100s (down from ~230s+ *inside* the combined process) — confirming the isolation hypothesis practically without confirming *why* isolation helps. That \"why\" is deliberately left open in [the next Open Issues entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000) as a narrower, lower-stakes curiosity now that the actual pain (an unaffordable per-turn cost) is gone.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0W09C8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T12:43:45.690Z",
    "title": "**Resolved**",
    "text": "**Resolved**: two compounding causes, not one. First, `repairLinkIntegrity` scoped its re-resolution to every link-bearing block in the affected artifact (`collectAllBlockNodes`) instead of just the blocks the discrepancy report actually names. Second, and more fundamental, it called `resolveBlockLinks` with `oldWikilinksByBlock` left `undefined` — the parameter that lets a freshly-resolved target reuse an existing `Link`'s id rather than minting a new one — so even a single touched block would have reminted every one of its own wikilinks, correct ones included. Fixed by grouping discrepancies by artifact, building `pendingLinks` only from the named blocks, and calling `collectOldWikilinksByBlock` (the same map `ingestFromDisk` already builds before its own `resolveBlockLinks` call) before repairing, so an already-correct wikilink keeps its id and only the genuinely-missing one mints fresh. Verified via `verify:slow`'s own step 19 scenario (one dropped link edge, repaired, discrepancies back to 0) and a clean corpus-wide `check-links` sweep afterward.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0XSZ8R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0XSZ8R001:props:StringProp:00CJ0XT270000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0XSZ8R001:props:StringProp:00CJ0XT270001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Resolved**",
    "text": "**Resolved**: two compounding causes, not one. First, `repairLinkIntegrity` scoped its re-resolution to every link-bearing block in the affected artifact (`collectAllBlockNodes`) instead of just the blocks the discrepancy report actually names. Second, and more fundamental, it called `resolveBlockLinks` with `oldWikilinksByBlock` left `undefined` — the parameter that lets a freshly-resolved target reuse an existing `Link`'s id rather than minting a new one — so even a single touched block would have reminted every one of its own wikilinks, correct ones included. Fixed by grouping discrepancies by artifact, building `pendingLinks` only from the named blocks, and calling `collectOldWikilinksByBlock` (the same map `ingestFromDisk` already builds before its own `resolveBlockLinks` call) before repairing, so an already-correct wikilink keeps its id and only the genuinely-missing one mints fresh. Verified via `verify:slow`'s own step 19 scenario (one dropped link edge, repaired, discrepancies back to 0) and a clean corpus-wide `check-links` sweep afterward.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CHYM4Z1G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
