  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ38WX0G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Resolved**",
    "text": "**Resolved**: the sweep is now standing, and two of its three triggers need nobody to remember them. The service re-checks the written artifact after every `update`/`insert`/`remove` and reports what that write resolved but failed to persist, on the write's own response; it re-checks the whole corpus at startup and on `reload`, carrying any finding on *every* later response until it clears (the same channel `contentConflict` uses, chosen because a lost link is invisible in every ordinary rendering and so has to arrive unasked-for). Only the end-of-batch corpus pass is left to discipline, because it alone catches damage that lands in a *different* artifact from the one edited — folded into the skill's *Stage each verified step* rather than given a step of its own. Verified live end to end: dropping one real link edge on disk and restarting produced the warning at startup, and that warning then surfaced on unrelated `tree` and `path` calls before `--repair` cleared it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJV8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ38Z8T0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**(3) Promoting a holder node can drop links from unrelated descendants elsewhere in the same artifact**",
    "text": "**(3) Promoting a holder node can drop links from unrelated descendants elsewhere in the same artifact**: `aperas update --text-only` calls whose only purpose was clearing a `.holder` flag on an `ArtifactNode`/H1/heading — no text change, no link-bearing block touched directly — left 8 list items several levels below empty of `.links`, none of them the target of any of the three writes and each previously confirmed to hold a real, resolved `Link`. `aperas check-links --repair` restored all 8 in one pass. Distinct from (1)/(2) in blast radius: the damage lands on nodes the write never touched at all, not just on the written node's own subtree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ3Z5DM0001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A42V8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:38.320Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A42V8001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A42V8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:39.111Z",
    "title": "**Link Integrity Checking built (`aperas check-links`)**",
    "text": "**Link Integrity Checking built (`aperas check-links`)**: resolved — the drift-style check has been built (`checkLinkIntegrity` and `repairLinkIntegrity` in `@aperas/core`), and its first landed version had a real gap, since fixed. It scans for live nodes where internal link codes exist in `node.text` but have no corresponding resolved `Link` in `.links`, and repairs them via the `--repair` flag, catching both historical staleness and transient regressions without needing to know the root cause (see [Open Issues](#id/BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001)). **Found on review, fixed**: the original match logic compared each text occurrence against `.links` by raw substring containment against the target's opaque id — reading as thorough (it correctly found and fixed 40 real historical discrepancies across 7 artifacts on its first live run) but actually sound only for codes that textually embed the id (`path#id/<ID>`, `aperas://id/<ID>`), which happens to be every code this corpus currently writes. It could not have verified `[[<deep-path>]]` addressing (`resolve.ts`'s deep-path grammar — a real, currently-live convention, \"used extensively\" in `Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`'s own self-references per `history/linking.md`) at all: a heading-path string shares no substring with the opaque id it resolves to, so a perfectly-resolved deep-path link would have been reported as missing. The one test meant to catch a genuine drop (deleting a `Link`'s `predicate` quad) also turned out to be vacuous — the match logic never reads `predicate`, and the scratch fixture already carried unrelated discrepancies of its own, so the assertion could never actually fail regardless of whether the deletion did anything (confirmed live: the count was identical before and after). **Actually fixed**: `checkLinkIntegrity` now resolves each occurrence for real via `resolveOneCode` (`artifacts.ts`, the same dispatch `resolveBlockLinks` itself uses, exported and called with `createHolder: false` so a scan can never mutate the graph as a side effect), and only reports a code that resolves to a live target with no matching entry in `.links` — an unresolved code is left alone, since that's routine (a bare `#fragment` anchor) or already `danglingRef`/`retryDanglingRefs`'s own separate concern. `verify.ts`'s step 19 replaced the vacuous predicate-quad deletion with a precise before/after discrepancy-count assertion against a real, freshly-resolved link.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A4FT0001",
