  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A4W08002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:40.693Z",
    "title": "**`extractAnchorNames` treats a quoted example anchor as a real claimed name — no code-span awareness**",
    "text": "**`extractAnchorNames` treats a quoted example anchor as a real claimed name — no code-span awareness**: `astParser.ts`'s `extractAnchorNames(text)` is a bare regex over a block's raw text, with no AST/markdown awareness, so an anchor tag written *inside backticks as an illustration* is indistinguishable from one the projector actually spliced in. `rejectSlugPathCollisions` then enforces the illustration as a real claimed name. Confirmed live: the `aperas` skill's own item 2 (SKILL.md) quotes a complete anchor tag to warn readers off pasting projected files back in, and that quoting alone made the block claim the address as its own — `BlockNode:00CE95MVP8008` holds it today. Only one claimant exists so far, so nothing has collided yet, but `design/linking.md` (the canonical anchor-syntax spec) is exactly the kind of document that must quote the same example, and would collide the moment it does. Not a judgement call: a code span is literal display text by definition, so an anchor inside one is never a real claim — the regex needs to skip backtick-delimited spans, mirroring how `findLeadInColonOffset` already excludes `inlineCode`. Full account in [discussion/aperas-skill.md](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001). **Resolved**, and widened while fixing it: a shared `anchorMatchesOutsideCode` helper now filters out any `ANCHOR_NAME_RE` match sitting inside a backtick span before either function sees it. Found designing the fix, not by this issue: `stripInlineAnchors` — this session's own new storage-time call included — had the identical gap in the opposite direction, and would *strip* a legitimately-quoted anchor example as if it were real projection markup, corrupting genuine prose. Both functions fixed from the one shared helper. Verified in isolation: a real anchor is still extracted/stripped, a quoted example is preserved untouched in both directions, and a mixed case (one real, one quoted) resolves each correctly.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGHVBF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A5850001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:23.657Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A5850001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGGG6N5G001 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGG6N5G001",
