  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A8RT8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:37.411Z",
    "title": "**Heading-in-list**",
    "text": "**Heading-in-list**: resolved — a block's title isn't limited to headings, which cap at H6 and don't fit an unbounded tree. Any block whose text opens with a colon-delimited lead-in term (`Term: rest of the text`) uses that term as its title; bold/`**...**` around the term is a free style choice, not part of the rule, and its `**` characters slugify like any other non-alphanumeric character if present, same as headings' `#` markers. List nesting carries unbounded depth the same way heading nesting carries bounded depth. See [Architecture](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8002). The exact rule for which colon counts as the delimiter — outside bold *and* inline code, length-capped, language-dependent — was refined after live corpus ingestion surfaced real corruption from a simpler version of it; see [Lead-In Term Detection](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG000).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
