  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0003:props:StringProp:00CJ6V6CHR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0003:props:StringProp:00CJ6V6CHR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Phase 1 is deep read, shallow write**",
    "text": "**Phase 1 is deep read, shallow write**: the whole read surface plus a single in-place text edit of the current node. This is the phase the settled design describes, and its write boundary is principled rather than a scope cut — one node's own text is the only change that needs no reconciliation and no structural judgment.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Phase 2+ is deep write**",
    "text": "**Phase 2+ is deep write**: composing an intent and handing it to the Agent. Unexamined, and deliberately not foreclosed by anything in Phase 1.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The ordering has a rationale**",
    "text": "**The ordering has a rationale**: core extraction comes first because both the CLI and the UI consume it, and because doing it while the CLI is still the only caller keeps the byte-identical text output as a free regression test. The auth token comes next because it is independent of the listener and is the one piece whose lifecycle should be proven before anything depends on it. The listener, the tick channel and the UI follow in that order, each with a working consumer by the time it lands. Revised: the tick channel (Slice 4) is deferred past the listener rather than following it immediately — it is the first long-lived connection this service would ever hold (everything else, including the listener, is one-request-one-response), so it carries new lifecycle surface (a stream registry, cleanup on disconnect, detecting a stale per-run token mid-stream) and a verification loop that requires killing the service mid-stream and watching timing-sensitive reconnect behavior, all disproportionate to Slice 4's own value once Slices 2/3 already work — the existing 1s poll keeps the app fully correct against the listener in the meantime, just less snappy. Slice 16 (packaging) takes its place immediately after the listener instead, since a working, packaged, testable end-to-end app is worth more sooner than a push channel layered onto one that doesn't exist yet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Task Breakdown",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0007",
