    "title": "**`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` is a stale, heuristic copy that can't be baked — found via a folder-listing link that stayed inert after the fix meant to cover it**",
    "text": "**`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` is a stale, heuristic copy that can't be baked — found via a folder-listing link that stayed inert after the fix meant to cover it**: `design/linking.md`'s own `**Discussion**:` line rendered `inline-link-inert` in the \"design\" folder's listing, even after the bare-relative-path resolver fix and `bakeResolvedLinkIds` render-time fix ([history/linking.md](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CJAVGNJG001), [history/webapp.md](../history/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CJAVRF90001), both 2026-09-18) were verified working for the direct case. Root cause: the folder-listing preview reads the `ArtifactNode`'s own `.text`, which is a *copy* of its first content-bearing descendant's text (`extractAbstract`), made once at hydration time — the descendant's real `Link` (confirmed already correctly resolved via `aperas show`) is never duplicated onto the container, so `bakeResolvedLinkIds` has nothing to bake with for this render path specifically. Deeper problem underneath: this copy is never refreshed by an ordinary deep edit either — `kgUpdate.ts`'s `target.text = text` never touches an ancestor's own `.text`, only a full re-hydration or the one-time holder-promotion path ever recomputes it — so the preview can go stale independent of this specific bug too. Direction agreed (see [discussion/core.md](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CKE4FFF0001)'s 2026-09-20 Freeflow entry for full rationale): retire the copy/heuristic entirely; give `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` a real `description` field in their own YAML frontmatter, with its own genuinely-owned `Link`s, edited via `aperas update` extended to accept `FolderNode` and to consume `frontmatter` — the corpus's long-standing \"update as a scoped reconciliation\" pattern, not a new bespoke channel. Implemented in `e66996b`, but only partly: the `description` prop and its bakeable links landed, while one of the two preview paths still reads the copy this issue exists to retire — see [the follow-on](#id/BlockNode:00CN7V9BX0001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKSAFHW0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-20T06:26:23.794Z",
    "title": "**Confirmed at real production scale, 2026-09-20**",
    "text": "**Confirmed at real production scale, 2026-09-20**: a corpus-wide re-ingestion left the entire service unresponsive for 40 minutes straight, not merely slow. `kg:migrate-frontmatter` (the [frontmatter-as-props redesign](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CKE4FFF0001)'s one-time pass, re-running `ingestFromDisk` against every live `ArtifactNode` with `bypassUnchangedCheck: true`) started 2026-09-20T05:14:39Z, ran continuously with the service process pinned at 150-280% CPU (confirmed still doing real work, never idle), and was still running with no end in sight when killed at approximately 2026-09-20T05:55Z, roughly 40 minutes in — for a corpus on the order of 50 artifacts, this is the same \"12-55 seconds per operation, compounding the longer the process has been alive\" pathology the entry above already names, stretched by scale from seconds into most of an hour. Root cause of the unresponsiveness, confirmed by reading `service.ts` directly rather than guessing: its migration loop calls `ingestArtifact` → `ingestFromDisk` → `reconcileTree` synchronously per artifact with no `await`/yield point anywhere in that path, and Node's single-threaded event loop cannot service *anything else* — a timer, a new socket connection, another CLI command — while a synchronous call is still on the stack. `service.ts`'s own 10-second auto-flush (`setInterval(() => enqueue(() => flushIfDirty()), 10_000)`, line 401-403) never got a single chance to fire for the whole run, confirmed directly: the JSON-LD mirror's own mtime never moved once during those 40 minutes. Independently corroborated live: a plain `aperas show <id>` issued from a separate terminal while the migration was still running failed with \"No ApeironNgn service running\" — not a crash, the event loop was simply never free to accept or process that new connection. Killed for an unrelated reason — a separate `reconcile.ts` bug this same run surfaced (`carryForwardFields` not re-syncing a fallback-titled block's `title` when it carries the *old* `blockId` forward, since `astParser.ts`'s own fallback sets `title` to a block's *fresh* scratch `blockId` when no real lead-in exists), not yet filed as its own entry — not because the operation had actually stalled.",
