    ],
    "title": "**The palette is explicit and dual: screen dark, print light**",
    "text": "**The palette is explicit and dual: screen dark, print light**: every theme color is a `--bg`/`--fg`/`--fg-muted`/`--border`/`--surface`/`--surface-hover`/`--accent` custom property on `:root` (`index.css`), never left to an inherited `color-scheme` default — the same discipline behind [full-brightness body text](#id/BlockNode:00CJE11FRR000): an implicit color is one half of a pairing that can silently disagree with its other half. Screen values (`--bg #26292b`, `--surface #313538`, `--fg #9ea4aa`, `--fg-muted #6b7278`, `--border #3a3d40`, `--accent #5aa7ff`) were sampled directly from the pixels of the Logseq UI this app is read alongside day to day (`UniinfoNotes/logseq/custom.css`), not eyeballed — the first pass matched a numerically-close but wrong pixel cluster (Logseq's page-title heading rather than its ordinary body text) and had to be corrected once compared by role, not just by value. `--accent` was never checked against Logseq this way; its hue happens to land within ~10° of Logseq's own `--accent` (`#0090ff`) regardless, apparently by common convergence on \"the obvious blue\" rather than by design. An `@media print` block on the same `:root` re-declares every variable for light-on-white (`--bg #fff`, `--fg #1b1e21`, `--accent #1a63c4`, ...) plus `color-scheme: light` and `print-color-adjust: exact`, so print never depends on the browser's own \"Background graphics\" toggle — that option only decides whether an authored background survives, and a page with no light palette of its own had nothing to fall back to once one was stripped. This only holds where a rule actually reads the variable: a literal hex bypasses both the Logseq match and the print fallback silently, which is how `.fd-link-title`, `.inline-link`, and the gutter's hover state each carried their own hardcoded blue until found by inspection.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMPNNP90001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The hover popover's open is delayed too, not just its close**",
    "text": "**The hover popover's open is delayed too, not just its close**: a quick pass over a link — reading down the list, not actually pausing on it — used to trigger the popover on every link the pointer crossed. `NavigableLink`'s `onEnter` (`Inline.tsx`) now schedules the same way `scheduleClose` already did, cancelled by a matching `cancelOpen` if the pointer leaves before it fires — 600ms, tuned to match this OS's own tooltip delay.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMPNQAVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Every row gets its own id-copy button, next to the edit pencil**",
    "text": "**Every row gets its own id-copy button, next to the edit pencil**: `CopyIdButton` (`FolderDiv.tsx`) copies that row's exact id — a node's own `n.id`, or a `Link`'s own `l().linkId` on every `LinkRow` mode, which had no pencil to sit next to at all — to the clipboard, swapping to `✅` for about a second first: unlike the pencil's own click (a visible edit box opens) or an unfold (a visible expansion), a clipboard write has no effect of its own to see, so leaving it silent would read as broken. Same hover-reveal styling as the pencil. The pencil itself was quietly illegible before this same pass too — `✎` (U+270E) renders as a thin, low-contrast outline glyph in most fonts — swapped for the two-tone `✏️` (U+270F+FE0F) emoji presentation, matching every other icon already in the row.",
