    "title": "00CN1PKAQG001",
    "text": "**`aperas update` without `--text-only` tombstoned a whole subtree, and the shutdown-time tombstone GC nearly made it permanent — 2026-09-22.** While recording [the ingestFromDisk scope-mismatch fix](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CN0MVG2R001) into `issues/linking.md`, updated the parent listItem (`BlockNode:00CGMWQ6T0001`, \"Links silently fail...\") with plain `aperas update`, no `--text-only`, piping bare text with no sub-list. Reconcile saw 0 piped children against 4 existing ones (items (1)-(4), two carrying their own nested \"Resolved\" notes) and tombstoned all 8 — even though the skill's own Mechanics section documents exactly this failure mode, worded around a *heading* target (\"Updating a heading — `--text-only`\"); the general principle (any container's `update` without `--text-only` reconciles away unlisted children) wasn't applied to this *listItem* target, many turns after that section was last read. A first recovery attempt, `aperas insert <tombstoned-id> --after <anchor>` (the identity-preserving move primitive), did not revive the node — confirmed live, `tombstonedAt` survives a move untouched — and additionally mis-positioned it as a sibling of its own former parent instead of a child. Backlink check then found two of the four items are real citation targets from elsewhere in the graph (`discussion/linking.md`'s Open Questions cites item (2); two blocks in this same Freeflow cite item (3)), so recreating fresh replacement content instead of reviving the originals would have left those citations dangling. On the user's own initiative, `git stash store $(git stash create)` snapshotted `AperasKG` immediately before the planned recovery (stop service, hand-edit the JSONLD mirror, restart) — which turned out to be essential: `aperas service stop`'s shutdown flush triggered the mark-and-sweep tombstone GC (`pruneUnreachableTombstones`), found the freshly-orphaned (parent-stripped) tombstoned nodes unreachable, and **permanently deleted** them before the hand-edit had even begun — including two nodes never accounted for in the recovery plan (an already-long-dead superseded draft, harmless to lose, and item (1)'s own live \"Resolved\" note, not harmless). Without the stash this would have been an unrecoverable loss beyond whatever could be reconstructed from memory. Recovered by restoring `Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` from the stash, then hand-editing it directly — removing `tombstonedAt` and restoring `parent`/`children` wiring for all 7 genuinely-live nodes — verified against `aperas show`/`backlinks`/`check-links --repair` (2 of the 7 had also lost their own resolved `Link`s, re-resolved cleanly), staged, stash dropped. Minor additional finding along the way: `check-links`'s \"found at startup\" cached warning is not cleared by a scoped `--repair` that fixes exactly the discrepancies it names — confirmed live that only a full corpus-wide sweep (`aperas reload`, or a restart) actually clears it, even though the repair itself is verifiably complete (`check-links` reports a clean pass in the very same response that still prints the stale warning). Two real, previously-undocumented tooling gaps this surfaced: no revive/untombstone operation exists anywhere in the service, and the shutdown-time GC can turn a same-session structural mistake into permanent loss with no grace window and no warning that a stop is about to collect anything — filed as [issues/core.md's Open Issues](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CJ0JZ830001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
