    ],
    "title": "**No revive/untombstone operation exists, and the shutdown-time tombstone GC can turn a same-session mistake into permanent loss with no grace window**",
    "text": "**No revive/untombstone operation exists, and the shutdown-time tombstone GC can turn a same-session mistake into permanent loss with no grace window**: confirmed live during recovery from [an accidental structural tombstone](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CN1PKAQG001) — `aperas insert <tombstoned-id> --after <anchor>` (the identity-preserving move primitive) repositions a tombstoned node but does not clear `tombstonedAt`, and no other verb in `service.ts`'s full op switch does either, so once a node is tombstoned there is no CLI-level path back short of hand-editing the JSONLD mirror. Worse, `aperas service stop`'s shutdown flush runs `pruneUnreachableTombstones` (the same mark-and-sweep GC documented at the other explicit-sweep call sites, `kg:reload`/`flush --clobber`) with no warning and no delay — a node tombstoned moments earlier in the very same session, if its `parent` has already been stripped (as an ordinary tombstone does), is indistinguishable from genuinely old, safely-collectible garbage and gets permanently deleted on the next stop/restart/reload. There is currently no cool-down, no confirmation, and no way to tell from the tombstoning operation's own output that a subsequent routine service restart will make the mistake unrecoverable. The only reason this specific incident was recoverable at all was an out-of-band `git stash` taken by the operator moments before the fatal restart — not anything the tooling itself provided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN1QEAGG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CN1QEAGG001",
    "text": "Design docs, dev status, and reports for the Aperas project, tracked as `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`\ncontent in ApeironNgn's own knowledge graph (`Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`) — this tree is both\ndocumentation and a live corpus the substrate ingests itself.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CN1QE950000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN4DGBQ0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A real, pre-existing slug-path collision in `planning/packaging.md`'s own Task Breakdown, latent since 2026-09-10, only surfaced by the first full re-ingest of the file in 12 days — 2026-09-22**",
    "text": "**A real, pre-existing slug-path collision in `planning/packaging.md`'s own Task Breakdown, latent since 2026-09-10, only surfaced by the first full re-ingest of the file in 12 days — 2026-09-22**: `aperas ingest planning/packaging.md --track --flush` (run as part of the corpus-wide frontmatter sweep) failed outright: `Full-slug-path collision: 'planning/packaging.md/--packaging---planning/---task-breakdown/root--package-json-' is already used by another live block (BlockNode:00CDH90MNG004) — rejected before writing anything.` Root cause: two separate Task Breakdown list items share the identical bold lead-in term `**Root `package.json`**` — item 3 (`BlockNode:00CDGV1RKR001`, \"workspaces field, updated `kg:*` script paths, dropped dead deps\") and item 9 (`BlockNode:00CDH90MNG004`, \"add an `\\\"aperas\\\": ...\\\" script\"`) — so both slugify to the exact same path. Traced via `git log -S` on each id: item 3 was introduced in `8dffd92` (2026-09-10 20:07:45, \"Package to monorepo/packages/{core,cli,web}\"), item 9 seventeen minutes later in `5b1d894` (2026-09-10 20:24:43, \"Package kg:<command> to `aperas <command>`; Add identity.json & aperas.config.json\") — the collision has existed in the graph since that second commit, invisible for twelve days because nothing had forced a full-body re-ingest of this specific artifact since (ordinary `aperas update`/`insert` calls at the block level never trigger `rejectSlugPathCollisions`, which only runs inside `ArtifactNode.ingestFromDisk`'s whole-document path). Both items already carry real, working id-anchors and resolve correctly via `aperas show`/`aperas tree` — this is purely a stale-slug-path hazard for any *future* full re-ingest of this one file, not a currently-broken read. Not fixed: fixing it means rewording one of the two lead-ins to be distinct (a content decision, not mechanical), out of scope for the frontmatter sweep that found it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ5ET5Z8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
