    ],
    "title": "**`design/aperas-skill.md` drifts silently against SKILL.md, because revisions strand where additions land and nothing on that edge can detect it**",
    "text": "**`design/aperas-skill.md` drifts silently against SKILL.md, because revisions strand where additions land and nothing on that edge can detect it**: measured 2026-09-22 — of design's 50 live blocks, 43 carry v1-authoring ids, 6 are v1.x-era and 1 v2-era with none later, while SKILL.md stands at v2.7; across the 137 blocks in the v1.1–v2.7 delta sections, 2 link into design. The failure is structural rather than disciplinary. An addition has an obvious home and writing it is the whole job, whereas a revision first requires *discovering* that some existing design block asserts what just changed — precisely the question the edit loop's deep-write step answers by backlinks, and precisely what SKILL.md cannot participate in, being a generative projection with no node and therefore no backlinks. Staleness cannot be recovered after the fact either: a BlockNode carries no last-edit time at all (`tombstonedAt` is the only timestamp present on any of the 1825 nodes), and an id encodes creation, surviving every later rewrite unchanged. All three existing instruments sit on the SKILL.md ↔ discussion edge — `scripts/skill_drift.py`, the v1 vertical thread (itself never rebuilt past v1.2), and the snapshot/delta record itself — which leaves the design edge with none. The remedy is two-way wiring, live now at [Citation direction](../design/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G) as the worked example: the design block declares the revision it reflects as a downward pointer — sanctioned by that section's own rule, being normative, singular, and replaced rather than accumulated — while the delta that revised it links back up, so staleness reduces to comparing two recorded facts, a declared version against the newest incoming delta link, instead of inferring from a timestamp the graph does not store. Remaining work: walk the 137 delta blocks, decide which of them revise something design actually asserts (many introduced new SKILL.md items with no design counterpart, and correctly link nowhere), wire those, and repair whatever design blocks turn out stale along the way — the wiring pass and the drift sweep are one walk, since a delta that should link up and doesn't is either an unwired edge or a stale block, with nothing to distinguish them until someone looks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
