    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW50VZ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWK33W0001",
    "text": "For an **ordered** list, direct insertion is riskier because a freshly inserted item becomes its own run-leader and can restart the numbering rather than continuing it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW4V5GG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0002",
      "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWKA6Q0001",
    "text": "To update the text of an *existing* list item without changing its identity, use `aperas update <item-id>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW4V5GG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0002",
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0003",
      "BlockNode:00CNX5YVV0001",
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0005",
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0006",
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW5Y57G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW5Y57G001:links:Link:00CNW5Y920000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CN8KBK9G002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW5Y57G001:links:Link:00CNW5Y920000:props:StringProp:00CNW5Y920001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Top matter — Status bumped to v2.10",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v2.9's own status line](#id/BlockNode:00CN8KBK9G002)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW4V5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CNW61XZG001",
      "BlockNode:00CP9RSEW0001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNW61XZG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Status",
    "text": "Status: **v2.10** — four levels, Philosophy through Mechanics, each item explaining a consequence of the one above it. Only current, verified items appear here; superseded material, unverified hypotheses and version-by-version rationale live in `discussion/aperas-skill.md`'s snapshot and deltas. Concern docs: `AperasKG/artifacts/{design,issues,planning,history,discussion}/aperas-skill.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW5Y57G001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0002:props:StringProp:00CNWK39NG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0002:props:StringProp:00CNWK39NG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CNWK33W0002",
    "text": "**Appending at the end is safe when the number is right.** For a list numbered `1, 2, …, n-1`, piping `n. new item` after the last one is accepted. Piping any other number is rejected outright, naming the number it expected instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW50VZ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWK33W0003",
    "text": "**A middle-of-the-list insertion still needs the full renumbering route** — the fix above only covers appending at the end, since inserting in the middle genuinely requires renumbering everything after it. The safer route there is to target the list's **parent heading** (`aperas update <heading-id>`) and pipe the heading line plus the *complete* list (every existing item verbatim plus the new one).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW50VZ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0004",
      "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0004:props:StringProp:00CNWK39NG002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0004:props:StringProp:00CNWK39NG003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CNWK33W0004",
    "text": "Exact-key matching reuses every unchanged item's id, **but only while every item keeps its parent and depth**. A push that changes item depth (e.g. regrouping under sub-headings) recreates everything; use `aperas insert` (moves) for that instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWK33W0005",
    "text": "**This only works if the piped content is genuinely complete.** Piping the heading plus *only* the new item reconciles the existing ones away as removed, tombstoning real content.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNWK33W0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0002:props:StringProp:00CNWKAC48000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0002:props:StringProp:00CNWKAC48001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CNWMVMBR001",
    "text": "**For an ordinary text change, pipe bare text with no bullet marker** (e.g. `updated text`, not `- updated text`). This also updates any nested children in the same call, if you include them — see the next point for the cases that need a marker instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWKA6Q0003",
    "text": "**A marker is needed only for a checkbox, an ordered item's position, or text that's deliberately blank.** Use whatever you'd normally write: `- [x]`/`- [ ]` for a checkbox, `n.` for an ordered item, or a bare `- ` (nothing after it) if you want the item's own text to end up empty while still giving it children:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWKA6Q0004",
    "text": "```\n  - \n    - child\n  ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0003",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWKA6Q0005",
    "text": "**Updating an item never changes where it sits in the list.** Whether it's ordered or unordered, and its position in the numbering, stays exactly as it was, no matter which marker you use to update it. To actually reorder or renumber, use the parent-plus-complete-list technique above — not a single-item update.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWQMSVG001",
    "text": "**The title always tracks your current text, never the old one.** If your new text has a bold lead-in term (`**Like this**: ...`), the title updates to match it. If it doesn't, the title falls back to the item's own id — the same fallback any untitled node gets — rather than keeping whatever the title used to say about text that's now gone.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKA6Q0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNWKA6Q0007",
    "text": "**The text itself works differently: leaving it out clears it.** If you only pipe new children (a list, with no text of its own before it), the item's own text becomes empty — unlike the title, nothing here is left alone by default. Restate the existing text if you don't want it wiped.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKKD80001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKKD80001:links:Link:00CNWKKGV0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKKD80001:links:Link:00CNWKKGV0000:props:StringProp:00CNWKKGV8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKKD80001:links:Link:00CNWKKGV8001",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CNWKKD80001:links:Link:00CNWKKGV8001:props:StringProp:00CNWKKGV8002",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "59"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Supersedes note — the old input-rule bullets",
    "text": "Supersedes: [The input rule](#id/BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002), [Explanation](#id/BlockNode:00CH5FJME8003)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW4V5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX0WDA8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-23T13:19:00.863Z",
    "title": "**`skill_drift.py` runs `norm()` before stripping a `Supersedes:` line, so the strip only works when that line is the block's entire text**",
    "text": "**`skill_drift.py` runs `norm()` before stripping a `Supersedes:` line, so the strip only works when that line is the block's entire text**: `subtree_texts` computes `body = norm(node.get(\"text\") or \"\")` first, then `SUPERSEDES_RE.sub(\"\", body)` — but `norm()` collapses newlines to spaces, and `SUPERSEDES_RE` is line-anchored (`^\\s*Supersedes:\\s*(.+)$`, multiline). Once the newline before \"Supersedes:\" has already been flattened into a space, there is no line boundary left for `^` to match, so the substitution is a silent no-op whenever a Supersedes line follows real content in the same text field — the bookkeeping stays embedded and poisons the fingerprint comparison. Confirmed live: appending `Supersedes: [...]` on its own line after a substantive sentence in one block's own `.text` made that block report `DROPPED, unaccounted` even though the *convention* (Supersedes as delta bookkeeping) says it shouldn't. The existing \"Top matter\" pattern elsewhere in this doc only avoids the bug by coincidence — its text is *nothing but* the Supersedes line, so it strips to empty and gets exempted by `subtree_texts`'s own `if body:` check before the ordering bug ever matters. Confirmed via direct reproduction against the actual functions (`skill_drift.norm`, `skill_drift.SUPERSEDES_RE`), not inferred. Not yet implemented: the fix is to strip before normalizing (or make `norm()` newline-preserving until after the strip runs), so a Supersedes line can safely follow real content in the same block instead of requiring a dedicated wrapper block every time.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX13PMR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`skill_drift.py` runs `norm()` before stripping a `Supersedes:` line, so the strip only works when that line is the block's entire text**",
    "text": "**`skill_drift.py` runs `norm()` before stripping a `Supersedes:` line, so the strip only works when that line is the block's entire text**: `subtree_texts` computes `body = norm(node.get(\"text\") or \"\")` first, then `SUPERSEDES_RE.sub(\"\", body)` — but `norm()` collapses newlines to spaces, and `SUPERSEDES_RE` is line-anchored (`^\\s*Supersedes:\\s*(.+)$`, multiline). Once the newline before \"Supersedes:\" has already been flattened into a space, there is no line boundary left for `^` to match, so the substitution is a silent no-op whenever a Supersedes line follows real content in the same text field — the bookkeeping stays embedded and poisons the fingerprint comparison. Confirmed live: appending `Supersedes: [...]` on its own line after a substantive sentence in one block's own `.text` made that block report `DROPPED, unaccounted` even though the *convention* (Supersedes as delta bookkeeping) says it shouldn't. The existing \"Top matter\" pattern elsewhere in this doc only avoids the bug by coincidence — its text is *nothing but* the Supersedes line, so it strips to empty and gets exempted by `subtree_texts`'s own `if body:` check before the ordering bug ever matters. Confirmed via direct reproduction against the actual functions (`skill_drift.norm`, `skill_drift.SUPERSEDES_RE`), not inferred. Not yet implemented: the fix is to strip before normalizing (or make `norm()` newline-preserving until after the strip runs), so a Supersedes line can safely follow real content in the same block instead of requiring a dedicated wrapper block every time.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX1T4XG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A corpus-wide `aperas ingest` (no path) can hang indefinitely on an interactive confirmation prompt with no signal that it's waiting rather than working**",
    "text": "**A corpus-wide `aperas ingest` (no path) can hang indefinitely on an interactive confirmation prompt with no signal that it's waiting rather than working**: `kg:ingest` run unscoped detected content it proposed to remove and printed `Are you sure you want to remove these? [yes/NO]:`, then blocked on stdin. Run from a non-interactive shell (no tty attached), that stdin is immediately at EOF, so the prompt never resolves — the process just sits there. Confirmed live: the process showed near-zero CPU the entire time (ruling out \"genuinely slow on a large corpus,\" which shows sustained high CPU instead, e.g. at service startup), and the ApeironNgn service itself answered an unrelated read (`aperas backlinks`) instantly throughout, proving the request never even reached the service's own queue — the block is entirely client-side, before `request()` is ever called. When stdin finally closed, the process exited 0 with the prompt still unanswered on screen; the flagged items turned out to already be old tombstones (dated a week prior), so nothing was actually lost this time, but nothing in the tool's own behavior guarantees that — an EOF being treated as \"no\" is incidental, not a documented contract. Not yet implemented: either a `--yes`/`--non-interactive` flag that skips the prompt with an explicit answer, or a `stdin.isTTY` check that hard-refuses (rather than blocking) when the answer can't actually be given.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX5SB9R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX5SB9R001:props:StringProp:00CNX5SBA8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX5SB9R001:props:StringProp:00CNX5SBA8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CNX5SB9R001",
    "text": "**A bare `- ` with real text but no `[x]`/`[ ]` removes an existing checkbox entirely** (e.g. `- updated text`, not `updated text`). Choosing list syntax at all is what signals \"this edit addresses the checkbox\" — a marker-less bullet clears it, while bare text (no bullet at all, the first point above) never touches it either way.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNX5YVV0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CNX5YVV0001",
    "text": "**A bare `- ` with real text but no `[x]`/`[ ]` removes an existing checkbox entirely** (e.g. `- updated text`, not `updated text`). Choosing list syntax at all is what signals \"this edit addresses the checkbox\" — a marker-less bullet clears it, while bare text (no bullet at all, the first point above) never touches it either way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW58YAG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9QJ9PG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
