        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "`aperas init` bootstraps a brand-new graph in the current directory",
    "text": "`aperas init` bootstraps a brand-new graph in the current directory: creates `aperas.config.json`, an empty `Apeiron/` store (`BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`/`Profile` `.jsonld`, each `[]`), and an `artifacts/` tree — refusing if a config already exists here, never overwriting an existing `.jsonld` file. Without it, `rehydrateStore` (`store.ts`) treats a missing instance file as a real `ENOENT` rather than an empty graph, and the service crashes on startup — which `aperas serve`/`aperas service start` can only report as a generic \"did not become ready in time\", giving no hint that the real problem is a directory that was never initialized. Found live installing the published package into a fresh project with no `Apeiron/` at all.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9QMPWR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "`aperas init` bootstraps a brand-new graph in the current directory",
    "text": "`aperas init` bootstraps a brand-new graph in the current directory: creates `aperas.config.json`, an empty `Apeiron/` store (`BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`/`Profile` `.jsonld`, each `[]`), and an `artifacts/` tree — refusing if a config already exists here, never overwriting an existing `.jsonld` file. Without it, `rehydrateStore` (`store.ts`) treats a missing instance file as a real `ENOENT` rather than an empty graph, and the service crashes on startup — which `aperas serve`/`aperas service start` can only report as a generic \"did not become ready in time\", giving no hint that the real problem is a directory that was never initialized. Found live installing the published package into a fresh project with no `Apeiron/` at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDHSJQE8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9QR9PG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "`aperas` shipped for real",
    "text": "`aperas` shipped for real: published on the public npm registry (`0.1.2` through `0.2.2`, `npm publish` from `packages/cli/dist/`) — anyone can `npm install -g aperas` today, not just build the bundle locally. Versioning follows its own scheme rather than semver-as-changelog: the middle number is a linear development-phase counter, independent of any other \"Phase N\" naming used elsewhere in this project, and the last is a build number within that phase — `0.2.1` is phase 2's first published build, `0.2.2` its second. `0.1.2` predates the scheme.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDW3R42G001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9QTHXR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CP9QTHXR001",
    "text": "The published package also carries this project's own two end-user-facing skills (`skills/aperas`, `skills/kg-doc-ingest` — this repo's other, dev-only skills are never bundled) copied into `dist/skills/` by the same build step, plus a new `aperas skill install` command (`kgSkill.ts`) that copies them into an agent's `.claude/skills/` (or `~/.claude/skills/` with `--global`). Without this, an agent working against an installed `aperas` binary has the CLI but none of the graph-first discipline the skill encodes — found live installing `aperas` into a project with no skill counterpart at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDW3R42G001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9QYPN8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
