    ],
    "title": "**2026-09-25: every hand-rolled markdown parser replaced with one shared `remark-gfm` AST**",
    "text": "**2026-09-25: every hand-rolled markdown parser replaced with one shared `remark-gfm` AST**: `MarkdownTable`/`MarkdownQuote`/`CodeBlock`'s fence detection/`Inline.tsx`'s inline tokenizer were four independent regex/string-walking re-implementations of the same spec `core`'s own `astParser.ts` already parses correctly — a real correctness risk (two independent decisions about what's a valid GFM table, free to disagree), not just duplication. New shared `markdown.ts` parses once via `unified`+`remark-parse`+`remark-gfm`; `classifyBlock` reads the resulting AST to detect table/quote/code/Mermaid instead of a bespoke sniffer per kind. Real wins beyond consistency: GFM strikethrough, nested formatting inside link text, and autolinks now render correctly, none of which the old tokenizer recognized; Mermaid detection is now `code.lang === 'mermaid'` instead of a diagram-type keyword list that didn't cover every type. `CodeBlock.tsx`'s own hand-rolled 5-language highlighter was also swapped for `highlight.js` (core + 21 explicitly registered languages, not the full ~190-language barrel — the 6 this corpus already uses plus 15 more common ones added ahead of need), since the old one had no entries for `json`/`rust`/`markdown`/`text` at all; an unregistered language now degrades to plain text rather than `highlightAuto` guessing among the registered set (confirmed live to mislabel Python as bash before Python was added). Both `MarkdownTable` and `CodeBlock` gained a Copy button; the table's is view-aware (TSV in \"Table\" view, Markdown in \"Source\" view). See [issues](../issues/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CQ0VT1NR001) for the full resolution.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6N9R003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
