    "title": "## v2.11 additions — delta on the v2 snapshot",
    "text": "Threaded onto [the v2 snapshot](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613VR001). Root `package.json`'s `kg:*` npm scripts were removed: once the `aperas` dispatcher covered every verb identically in dev (`npm run aperas -- <verb>`) and in the packaged binary, they were a second command surface doing exactly the same work as the first, not a distinct dogfooding workflow — see [design/packaging.md's Topology](../design/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDGTZHSR005), which supersedes its own prior rationale for keeping them. Runtime `console.log`/`console.error` prefixes across every `kgX.ts` file, plus the flush/reload conflict-resolution hint (`serviceProtocol.ts`'s `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_HINT`), were rewritten from `[ApeironNgn kg:X]`/`npm run kg:X` to `[aperas X]`/`npm run aperas -- X` to match — the `--help` usage lines had already been rewritten in an earlier pass ([history/packaging.md](../history/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CDY84BJ8002)), but these runtime messages had been missed until now. This skill's own one remaining `kg:*` mention, in the `check-links` Mechanics entry below, is closed the same way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZJR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50002",
      "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50003",
      "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Mechanics — `check-links` dispatch corrected to `aperas`",
    "text": "Answers exactly the question dense linking depends on and nothing else routinely checks: does every internal-style reference a live block's text actually names (`[[code]]`, `aperas://...`, `path#fragment`) have a matching resolved `Link` in that block's own `.links`? `aperas check-links` reports discrepancies; `aperas check-links --repair` re-resolves and flushes them in the same call. It resolves each occurrence for real (the same dispatch `aperas update`/`aperas insert` themselves use, read-only here — never mints a placeholder as a side effect of a scan) rather than guessing from the text, so it correctly stays silent on a code that simply doesn't resolve yet (routine, or already tracked separately as a dangling reference) and only flags a code that resolves to a live target with nothing to show for it in `.links` — the exact, previously-invisible failure mode this tool exists for.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
