          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50004:links:Link:00CQYGPV48002:props:StringProp:00CQYGPV48003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### Top matter — Status bumped to v2.11",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v2.10's own status line](#id/BlockNode:00CNW61XZG001)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Status",
    "text": "Status: **v2.11** — four levels, Philosophy through Mechanics, each item explaining a consequence of the one above it. Only current, verified items appear here; superseded material, unverified hypotheses and version-by-version rationale live in `discussion/aperas-skill.md`'s snapshot and deltas. Concern docs: `AperasKG/artifacts/{design,issues,planning,history,discussion}/aperas-skill.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CQYGPR50004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3WYBM8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`root` records the current zoom**",
    "text": "**`root` records the current zoom**: a view's `root` is the apex `aperas tree --view` renders from when no apex is given, and every zoom — an agent re-rooting a view for a hand-off, or a human zooming in the webapp — overwrites it. Only the current root is recorded, not a history of past ones. Unset means the whole corpus, as before `root` existed. Rooting a view on a dashboard node is what turns a dashboard from a list of links into a working lens: the reader sees exactly the scope the author gathered under that node, reached through its children and links, with the unfold state on top of that deciding how deep each part opens.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4Y800B",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3WYRG8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Threads persist to `.state/` by membership, not by class**",
    "text": "**Threads persist to `.state/` by membership, not by class**: every node inside an `artifacts/.threads/` artifact's subtree — the ArtifactNode, its BlockNodes and their links — is dehydrated into `.state/` beside `TreeView`, never into the tracked content mirror, because an agent's thread is working state as per-agent and as ephemeral as a view. They are ordinary `ArtifactNode`/`BlockNode`s otherwise, so every existing verb (`unfold`, `show`, `update`, `insert`, `remove`) works on them unchanged, and pruning is an ordinary `remove` under the existing tombstone mechanism, with no separate deletion path. Nothing outside a thread links into one, which is what makes the split safe: the tracked mirror never holds a reference into state it doesn't carry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG00C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Multi-agent working",
    "text": "Three roles share one graph: an **orchestrator** that talks with the user, a **grapher** that works the graph, and a **coder** that works the code. One agent carrying the whole skill alone drops steps, because the loop has outgrown what a single context reliably keeps in hand, so the work is split by what each role writes. Every role loads the same base skill, since the graph is both the substrate of all the work and the medium the roles communicate through.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5J8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00002",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08001",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08002",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08004",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08006",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Roles and write scope",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00003",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00004",
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00003:props:StringProp:00CR3X49DR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00003:props:StringProp:00CR3X49DR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Orchestrator**",
    "text": "**Orchestrator**: records the conversation with the user as `discussion` freeflow — creating the topic's `## Freeflow` when it has none — decides what happens next, and hands work to a subagent. It is gated upfront: never investigate or modify code on your own at task start. After that it may read code, diffs, and tests to check, assess or review a result or a claim, as in Step 7 Verification; mapping what the code does stays with the coder. At session start or when resuming, run the in-flight triage protocol (`aperas profile list <p>`, inspect `<p>-coder-orchestrator` and `<p>-grapher-orchestrator`). It writes only freeflow and its own turns in the subagent threads, and stages those writes itself, as every role stages what it writes for its own recovery. It reads the graph and the code directly to check, assess or review a result or a claim before relaying it, and whatever it relays goes into freeflow first, citing its source; a task's first read of the graph and its first map of the code stay with the grapher and the coder. Its own upline is the user: when a hand-off, command, service or test run has not answered after one minute, it reports that to the user and may keep waiting only after telling the user, and a restart or kill of the service is for the user to approve.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Grapher**",
    "text": "**Grapher**: does every other graph operation — deep read to find what is relevant, deep write to update it, every promotion (thread to `concepts.md` or higher, discussion to the formal docs), and each thread's lifecycle. It is the only role that writes the graph at large.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Coder**",
    "text": "**Coder**: surveys and changes source code. It reads the graph for context and writes only to its own thread.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### The loop",
    "text": "A task runs a fixed 9-step sequence of turns, grapher always first: grapher surveys the graph, coder surveys the code without changing it, grapher files that survey, coder changes the code, the orchestrator verifies the diff and the tests itself, grapher files the result — repeated as needed, followed by the finishing turn. The orchestrator is gated upfront against investigating or modifying code at task start. When resuming in-flight work, the orchestrator triages views (`<p>-coder-orchestrator`, `<p>-grapher-orchestrator`) before dispatching new turns. Each survey stays on its own side: grapher touches source code only to pinpoint an entry into the graph, and an Ask asks only for its receiver's kind of work. Every Ask cites the freeflow nodes for the decisions it relies on, so a decision that exists only in chat blocks the hand-off until it is recorded. Before dispatch the orchestrator runs each Ask against a short checklist of the rules most often broken while one is being written — only the receiver's kind of work, every decision cited from freeflow, cite rather than retype, one task per dispatch — since nothing else raises them at that moment. Each hand-off can run several turns, the subagent asking back when unsure and the orchestrator asking follow-ups or, after verifying, for fixes. Coder's findings reach the formal graph only through grapher, which promotes a finding that is a defect or a gap to an `issues/` entry in the Filing Turn that records it, while promotions of recurring patterns to `concepts.md` and of settled outcomes to design and history happen in each task's finishing step.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Communication — views carry data, chat carries control",
    "text": "A message between roles is a call: the view is its shared context, like `this`, and the message can still name specific nodes, links and questions as further arguments. Chat tracks progress; the view tracks the current state of the work. A subagent's continuity between turns is its thread plus its views rather than its chat history, so the design holds on a platform that does not keep a subagent alive between invocations.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08003:links:Link:00CR3X49ER000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CR3WYBM8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08003:links:Link:00CR3X49ER000:props:StringProp:00CR3X49ER001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "353"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CR3X3N08003",
    "text": "A dashboard is where a view is projected from. Between user and orchestrator it is the discussion doc's `## Dashboard`; between orchestrator and a subagent it is that subagent's thread. Each turn the sender adds only the nodes and links that changed, creates a new root node citing them alongside the content already there, and re-roots its view on it ([TreeView root](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CR3WYBM8001)). The root moves every turn while the content beneath it is mostly reused.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Threads",
    "text": "Each subagent has one thread, `artifacts/.threads/<role>.md` — `grapher.md`, `coder.md` — one per role while work is not parallel. A thread is a tree of topic (named by the user) > task (set by the orchestrator) > turn (one exchange with the orchestrator) > subturn. Every turn has subturns, at least one per batch of tool calls: the turn's Work line holds the approach in a single line and cannot hold the results, so each subturn records its batch's progress and reasoning and the essence of each tool result a later turn will need, written as it happens or at the end of the turn. A result left unrecorded is gone for every later turn, since the thread, not the chat, is what carries context forward. A subturn also records the friction it hit, which is any trouble in the turn — its own mistake, a tool-use problem, or a tool gap, with the workaround built, the call that failed or hung, or the retry and what changed — and the Report lists each as its own `Friction — <topic>` entry, and each open question as its own `Question — <topic>` entry, since sibling slugs derive from the lead-in and two identical lead-ins collide and reject the whole write. The grapher files a real tool gap under the tool's issue; an own mistake or a tool-use problem stays in the task's freeflow. Waiting is bounded the same way for every role: a turn that meets a command, service or process that does not answer stops after one minute in total, records it as a `Friction — <topic>` entry and ends with a `Question — <topic>` to the orchestrator, and a subagent that finds the service down ends its turn without restarting it, because the orchestrator owns the service. A topic is a concern the user names — usually one concern's artifact set, sometimes spanning concerns, or a batch of related tasks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005:links:Link:00CR3X49ER002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005:links:Link:00CR3X49ER002:props:StringProp:00CR3X49ER003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "35"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005:links:Link:00CR3X49ER004",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CR3WYRG8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08005:links:Link:00CR3X49ER004:props:StringProp:00CRAZQF78000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "325"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CR3X3N08005",
    "text": "Threads are the lowest tier of the [citation gradient](#id/BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G), so a thread cites upward and threads cite each other sideways, while nothing above the threads tier links into one, and promotion copies content upward rather than citing it. Their graph content persists to the gitignored `.state/` mirror ([thread persistence](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CR3WYRG8001)). Every write to a thread checks it against a size cap and warns once the cap is reached; grapher then prunes it with an ordinary `remove`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Profiles and view naming",
    "text": "Per platform `<p>` (`claude`, `antigravity`), three profiles: `<p>`, `<p>-grapher`, `<p>-coder`. Views facing the user are `<p>-<topic>`. The orchestrator's views facing each subagent are `<p>-orchestrator-grapher` and `<p>-orchestrator-coder`; each subagent's view facing the orchestrator is `<p>-grapher-orchestrator` or `<p>-coder-orchestrator`. Each thread therefore carries two views, one from each side.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N08007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Skill packaging",
    "text": "One shared base skill — working the graph — loaded by every role, plus one role skill each for orchestrator, grapher and coder, stating only what that role writes, when it hands off, and how. The task loop itself lives in the base skill, so every role sees the whole sequence around its own turns; each role skill details only its own. Each subagent is an agent definition that preloads the base and its role skill: `.claude/agents/<name>.md` for Claude Code, `.agents/agents/<name>.md` for Antigravity, both shipped by `aperas skill install`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR3X3N00001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3XH5DG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3XH5DG000:props:StringProp:00CR3XH880000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3XH5DG000:props:StringProp:00CR3XH880001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Concepts Doc**",
    "text": "**Concepts Doc**: `artifacts/concepts.md`, one concept map shared across every concern — the vocabulary and relationships the work keeps reusing, promoted up from discussion and from agent threads. It ranks above discussion and below the formal docs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDD68E80007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR3XJX5G001",
