  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D1WW0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CR9D1WW0002",
    "text": "```markdown\n# Grapher thread\n## <topic>                                   ← named by the user\n### <task>                                   ← set by the orchestrator\n#### Turn <n>                                ← one exchange with the orchestrator\n- **Ask**: <what is wanted, in a sentence>   ← orchestrator; root of <p>-orchestrator-grapher\n  - [<argument>](../design/x.md#id/BlockNode:…) — why it matters here\n- **Work**: <the approach, in a line>        ← subagent; subturns nested under it\n  - <subturn>\n- **Report**: <the outcome, in a sentence>   ← subagent; root of <p>-grapher-orchestrator\n  - [<changed or found node>](…) — what changed, or what it shows\n  - **Question**: <whatever the Ask left unsettled — see below>\n  - **Tool gap**: <a workaround built, a call that failed or hung — see below>\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR9D1WW0001",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D5Z60001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### aperas — Discipline — Threads, record the friction not only the results",
    "text": "**Record the friction, not only the results.** A subturn also records every place the tools got in the way: a workaround you built (a script, a loop over a command, a detour through another verb), a call that failed, hung or had to be stopped, each retry and what changed in it, an error you routed around, a write that did something you didn't intend. Give the command and what happened, even once you got past it — especially then, because a problem you solved leaves no trace in the results, and nobody will remember it after the turn. The Report lists each one as a **Tool gap**, so the grapher can file it. A gap that is not in the thread is one the graph can never notice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR874TC8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR001:links:Link:00CR9D7KHG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CR874TCG00A",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR001:links:Link:00CR9D7KHG000:props:StringProp:00CR9D7KHG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "12"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "### aperas-orchestrator — Every exchange with the user, friction recorded too",
    "text": "Supersedes: [the missing-Freeflow entry](#id/BlockNode:00CR874TCG00A)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR874TC8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Record it in the topic's discussion `## Freeflow` before acting on it",
    "text": "Record it in the topic's discussion `## Freeflow` before acting on it: what was asked, what was decided, what was corrected, and why. The conversation can be compacted or cut off at any point, and a decision that only ever existed as chat is gone the moment that happens, with no way to resume or hand it off from what is on disk. Freeflow is raw material — record as you go, not once things are resolved; the grapher organizes and promotes it. If the topic's discussion has no `## Freeflow` yet, create it. Friction you hit yourself — a workaround, a call that failed or hung, a write that did something you didn't intend — goes into freeflow too, the way a subagent records it in its subturns (base: *Threads*).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR9D7DVR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CR9D8210001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
