    "title": "**A command can fail with \"No ApeironNgn service running\" while the service is up, and an unchanged retry works**",
    "text": "**A command can fail with \"No ApeironNgn service running\" while the service is up, and an unchanged retry works**: recorded in two separate grapher Reports. The first `aperas insert` of one turn failed with that message while `aperas service start` immediately reported the service already running (pid 220722); the same message came back later in that turn on `aperas project issues/linking.md`, and in a second turn on an `aperas update` between otherwise working commands. Every unchanged retry succeeded with no restart. Unlike [the silent death below](#id/BlockNode:00CRBJ0VYG001), the service never went down. A likely cause, read from the code and consistent with the load observation at the end: `ensureServiceRunning` (`packages/cli/src/apeironNgn/serviceClient.ts:149`) throws that message whenever `ping()` returns false, and `ping()` (line 83) turns every error, a timeout included, into `false` after `PING_TIMEOUT_MS = 300`, so a service busy with a flush or an ingest and slow to answer within 300 ms reads as not running. Fix to try: retry the ping once or twice, or raise the timeout, before throwing, and say in the message when a socket exists but did not answer. Reproduced by load later the same day, in the v4.3 filing sweep: while the service was busy after an `aperas remove --flush` of a finished thread task (the process was alive at 100 to 290 percent CPU for minutes), every command, `aperas show` included, failed with that message, and the same commands worked once the service was idle again, which fits a 300 ms ping reading a busy service as down. Two earlier observations point the same way: `aperas service start` answered \"Already running\" with the old pid right after a command failed, and `service.log` holds only startup lines, with no shutdown, flush or connect-failure line, so it cannot say why a client could not reach a live service. Not tested: a concurrent second client as the cause of the earlier drops. Mechanism, from the investigation's code survey of 2026-10-02 (read from `service.ts`, `serviceClient.ts` and `kgService.ts`; the failures are consistent with it, and no direct test has isolated it): every request, `ping` included, enters `handleConnection`, is chained onto the single promise `queue` by `enqueue`, and runs `handle`, which has no `await`, so each operation is one uninterrupted synchronous run. A `ping` therefore waits behind the operation in flight and every request already queued ahead of it, and the client's `ping()` gives up after `PING_TIMEOUT_MS = 300`; the 300 ms bound covers queue wait plus the ping's own handling, not whether the loop is stuck. Any operation over about 0.3 s, or a queue of two or three, makes the next command say \"No ApeironNgn service running\" with nothing stalled. Every other command first calls `ensureServiceRunning`, which is that one 300 ms ping, and `request()` itself sends with no timeout, so a command that passes the ping waits as long as the service takes. The sampler's recorded failures fit it without a stall (read, not proven): of its 11 failed probes, three are at 5, 12 and 19 percent CPU with 2 to 4 `aperas` clients running (21:11:24, 21:14:24, 21:15:24), where another client's operation or flush was probably queued ahead of the probe; the sampler keeps only whether the answer held `\"@id\"` and discards the failure text, so it cannot tell the ping failure from a request error or a timeout (see [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001)). What the queue takes depends on what the queue holds: [every write re-parses its whole artifact twice](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001), [a task removal runs a full-store scan per embedded node](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000), and [boot does seconds of synchronous work before the socket exists](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001). Untested: how much of a failed ping's 300 ms is queue wait against the in-flight operation, and the fix directions above against a counted run of the same load. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMPB2Y0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`skill_drift.py` prints every unit cut to 100 characters (80 for the accounted-for list) and has no flag to print one in full**",
    "text": "**`skill_drift.py` prints every unit cut to 100 characters (80 for the accounted-for list) and has no flag to print one in full**: `skills/aperas/scripts/skill_drift.py` cuts each unrecorded and each unaccounted-dropped unit to `b[:100]` (lines 291 and 310) and each accounted-for one to `b[:80]` (line 319), but the units it compares run to several hundred characters, and what separates an old unit from its replacement is usually past the cut. Matching an added unit to the dropped block it replaces, which is the whole of recording a delta, therefore needed a scratch script importing `file_units`, `subtree_texts` and `superseded_ids` in each of the two turns that recorded the v4.3 edit (24 added and 13 dropped, then 19 and 10). Fix: a `--full` flag that prints each unit untruncated, and the Grapher skill's \"Order of work\" line, which now states the two lengths, drops them. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMPD5PG001",
