    "title": "**A thread-task `aperas remove` can leave the service CPU-bound for many minutes, during which every command fails and a graceful stop does not complete**",
    "text": "**A thread-task `aperas remove` can leave the service CPU-bound for many minutes, during which every command fails and a graceful stop does not complete**: found 2026-10-02 pruning finished tasks from `.threads/grapher.md` and `.threads/coder.md` in the v4.3 skill task. Of 15 removals, most returned in 2 to 10 seconds. After removing `memory-rules-and-orientation-restructure` (42 live blocks) the service answered nothing for about 5.4 minutes (tombstone at 11:37:44Z, next success at 11:43:07Z), and after `promote-and-fix-subagent-tool-gap` (31 live blocks) it stayed unreachable for about 12 minutes (tombstone at 11:44:10Z, first answer at about 11:56:40Z); tasks of 39 and 50 blocks returned quickly, so size alone does not predict it. Throughout, the process was alive at 100 to 290 percent CPU and 3.4 to 4.6 GB resident, `service.log` recorded nothing, and every command, `aperas show` included, failed with \"No ApeironNgn service running\", which is the symptom of the service-ping issue above. A later `aperas service restart` sent SIGTERM and failed with \"didn't stop within 10s of SIGTERM\"; the old process was still alive more than 40 minutes later, when it was killed. On the fresh service started afterwards (about 1 GB resident) three further removals took 2, 2 and 45 seconds and the next command could not reach the service for minutes, so age and heap size alone do not explain the stall. Each removal also leaked its descendants' tombstones into the tracked `Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` (see the leak issue in `issues/treeview.md`), and `pruneUnreachableTombstones` hard-deletes each unreachable tombstone one at a time at shutdown and startup, which is the likely cost; not tested. Hypothesis, not a finding: this is the process-lifetime slowdown already recorded in [the `npm run verify` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001) and [its open \"why\" entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000), a long-lived node process that degrades, here the service at about 5 GB resident after about three hours, where a process split is not an option because the service is the shared store. The differences: the earlier slowdown showed up as steps running 12 to 55 seconds instead of hundreds of milliseconds, with oxigraph at roughly 1 GB, not as multi-minute stalls with no answer at all; the repeat on a fresh process fits poorly with a slowdown that builds up over a process's lifetime, though degradation within a few minutes of heavy work would still match; and the earlier entry's leading guess was a JIT-level degradation, which this entry does not test. If it is the same mechanism, the profiling that entry proposes (`--prof` or `--trace-deopt`) is the next step, run against the service. A second suspected cause, untested: every `remove`, `insert`, `update` and `retype` runs `checkArtifactLinkIntegrity(store, artifactId)` before the operation and again in `withLinkCheck` after it, over the whole artifact (read from `service.ts`), so one `remove` of a task inside a 600-block thread file costs two full link-integrity sweeps on top of the removal itself. The verify-slowdown issues, [the `npm run verify` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001) and [its open \"why\" entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000), name the same kind of operation, `checkLinkIntegrity`'s corpus sweep, as the one that went from low hundreds of milliseconds fresh to 12 to 55 seconds late in a long process, so the sweeps are a candidate for the removal stall. A lead, not a finding: no test has separated the sweeps from the tombstone hard-deletes above. Workaround: wait, retry the command unchanged, and do not kill the process, because the thread tier's `.state/` mirror had not been flushed since before the removals, which is [its own issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVNZXVR8001): killing the service lost every removal. Mechanism from the investigation's code survey of 2026-10-02, a third candidate that the \"hard-deletes one at a time\" sentence above only names (read from `service.ts` and the store code; the cost of one scan measured in a scratch process; no run has attributed a stall to it): for a task `T` in artifact `A`, one `aperas remove --flush` runs, synchronously and in this order, `artifactForRef`, the pre-check sweep of `A`, `runRemove`, the content flush, and the post-check sweep of `A`. `runRemove` calls `tombstoneLiveSubtree`, which for each block depth-first sets `children = []`, `links = undefined`, `props = undefined` and `tombstonedAt`; the `links` and `props` assignments go through `writeField`'s embed branch, which calls `hardDeleteNode` on every embedded `Link` and `StringProp` (recursing into each `Link`'s position props), and each `hardDeleteNode` begins with `removeDanglingUnfolds`, which calls `allIdsOfKind(store, 'TreeView')`, a `store.match` over every quad in the store. One such scan measured 136 to 144 ms at 39.6k quads. The cost of a removal therefore follows the number of embedded nodes under the removed task, about that count times 140 ms, and not its block count, which fits the pattern above (39 and 50 blocks fast, 42 and 31 slow): a task whose blocks carry many wikilinks has many `Link` nodes and position props, while a plain list has few. The deferred half (read, with the per-node cost measured): at the next reload, shutdown or startup `pruneUnreachableTombstones` finds the removed blocks unreachable and calls `hardDeleteNode` on each, another full scan each, about 6 s for 42 blocks (derived) on top of its fixed 2.3 s, which is the cost described in [the boot and shutdown issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001). The whole-artifact sweeps in the second suspected cause are now measured at 1.7 to 2.5 ms a live block, so about 0.6 to 0.9 s a sweep on a 367-block thread file, and they are real, but two of them cost seconds (derived), not the minutes seen, and [their own issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001) carries them. Not shown: the 5.4 and 12 minute stalls would need about 2,300 and 5,000 such scans at 140 ms, and whether those tasks held that many embedded nodes is not known; the coder's run of an in-memory removal of the heaviest coder tasks in a fresh process did not finish in 100 s, consistent with the mechanism but not attributed to a step. Untested: the whole mechanism against a stall, which needs the per-operation timings and a profile during a stall that [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001) lists. The scan also allocates a JavaScript `Quad` per store quad on every call (about 40k objects, 140 ms), which is a candidate source of the garbage-collector pressure and the external-memory growth in [the memory issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQ4Z968001) (read, untested). Filed 2026-10-02.",
