    "title": "**Hypothesis — the stall is the same mechanism as the earlier process-lifetime slowdown**",
    "text": "**Hypothesis — the stall is the same mechanism as the earlier process-lifetime slowdown**: (user: \"This sweep, I think, links to the issue of the very long run of `verify` which forced us to split it to `verify:slow`\") The issue [verify slowdown](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001) recorded a process-lifetime slowdown that only a fresh process reset (oxigraph's WASM footprint at about 1 GB and V8 heap size were ruled out), and [why isolation helps](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000) says to profile with `--prof` or `--trace-deopt` if it recurs where a process split is not viable. The service is a long-lived process where a split is not viable. Differences: the earlier slowdown was slower steps, not multi-minute stalls, and the footprint is now about 5 GB. Not tested. A test that separates the two causes: time one large `remove` on a fresh service and the same `remove` after a long uptime.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVK45ZQ0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVNZXVR8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A write to a thread node never marks the `.state/` mirror dirty, so it exists only in memory until a view change or a shutdown sweep, and a killed service loses it**",
    "text": "**A write to a thread node never marks the `.state/` mirror dirty, so it exists only in memory until a view change or a shutdown sweep, and a killed service loses it**: found 2026-10-02 when the service stuck after the pruning removals (see the removal-stall entry above) was killed. In `packages/cli/src/apeironNgn/service.ts`, `insert`, `update`, `remove` and `retype` set only `dirty`, whose `flushIfDirty` writes the tracked content mirror, and that mirror excludes every `.threads/`-scoped node; the thread-scoped `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` records are written only by `flushStateIfDirty` into `.state/`, which does nothing unless `stateDirty` is set, and only the `unfold`, `fold`, `zoom` and profile ops, the garbage-collection sweeps and `shutdown`'s own sweep set it. Evidence: `.state/BlockNode.jsonld` was last written at 18:26:20 while 15 `aperas remove --flush` calls, each reporting success, ran after it; every earlier turn's thread writes persisted only because the turn ended with `aperas zoom`. After the kill, the fresh service rehydrated the stale `.state/` records of the removed tasks beside their tombstoned copies that had leaked into the tracked mirror (see the leak entry in `issues/treeview.md`), logged \"323 duplicate @id(s) in the mirror\", merged them, and listed the removed task roots as live again with their descendants tombstoned. The gap is a routing mismatch, not a timer-cadence one, and both timers (3 s and 10 s) work as designed: a thread-scoped node is routed to the `.state/` mirror when the store is dehydrated (`THREAD_PARTITIONED_CLASSES`, `isThreadScopedId`), but the dirty flag is chosen without that partition, so the four handlers set `dirty`, which feeds the content flush that excludes thread nodes, and never `stateDirty`, which feeds the `.state/` flush that includes them. History, from git: the `.state/` mirror and `dehydrateStateToJsonLd` arrived on 2026-09-03 with the TreeView refactor (`e4cc806`), for views only, so no node record needed it; `stateDirty` followed in the 09-15 and 09-17 commits (`96ed8f9`, `0624b7d`); the thread partition arrived on 2026-09-27 with the multi-agent v3.0 commit (`b58dc5d`), which added the `zoom` op with `stateDirty = true` and one `dirty = true` and did not touch the four handlers, so the thread tier inherited a flush path built for views. Fix, agreed 2026-10-02 as three parts that fold all node-related persistence into `.state/`: (1) any write to a thread-scoped node sets `stateDirty` (`isThreadScopedId` is already imported in the same file), so the 3-second timer and every `--flush` persist it; (2) the `.state/` divergence stamps cover the thread-partitioned node classes and not only `STATE_CLASSES` (`TreeView`), so an external change to the thread `BlockNode` file there is noticed (not checked: that an external change goes unnoticed today); (3) every flush path writes both mirrors from one rule. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
