        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CVPK8PA8000:props:StringProp:00CVPK8Y60000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Finding — the thread-flush gap is a routing mismatch, not a timer-cadence one**",
    "text": "**Finding — the thread-flush gap is a routing mismatch, not a timer-cadence one**: (user: \"the gap is the behavior mismatch between the 3s flush to `.state` and the main 10s flush, isn't it?\") Both timers work as designed and their cadences (3 s and 10 s) are not the defect. The mismatch is that a thread-scoped node is routed to the `.state/` mirror when the store is dehydrated (`THREAD_PARTITIONED_CLASSES`, `isThreadScopedId`), but the dirty flag is chosen without that partition: `insert`, `update`, `remove` and `retype` set `dirty`, which feeds the content flush that excludes thread nodes, and never `stateDirty`, which feeds the `.state/` flush that includes them. The fix keeps one rule in both places, setting `stateDirty` when a write touches a thread-scoped node. Possibly related, not checked: the `.state/` divergence check stamps only `STATE_CLASSES` (`TreeView`), so an external change to the thread `BlockNode` file there would not be noticed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVQ1GWQ8001"
    ]
  },
