      }
    ],
    "title": "**Finding — the thread-flush gap is a history gap**",
    "text": "**Finding — the thread-flush gap is a history gap**: (user: \"the first `.state` had contained no nodes (only views), so `stateDirty` had not set, but then the threads came without updating that behavior\") Git history agrees. The `.state/` mirror and `dehydrateStateToJsonLd` arrived on 2026-09-03 with the TreeView refactor (`e4cc806`), for views only, and `stateDirty` followed in the 09-15 and 09-17 commits (`96ed8f9`, `0624b7d`). The thread partition (`THREAD_PARTITIONED_CLASSES`, thread-scoped `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` going to `.state/`) arrived on 2026-09-27 with the multi-agent v3.0 commit (`b58dc5d`). That commit added the `zoom` op with `stateDirty = true` and one `dirty = true`, and did not touch the `insert`, `update`, `remove` and `retype` handlers, which still set only `dirty`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVQ1J088000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVPQFW6G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open — what \"fold all node-related stuff into `.state`\" means**",
    "text": "**Open — what \"fold all node-related stuff into `.state`\" means**: (user: \"the fix should fold all node-related stuff into `.state`\") The orchestrator reads it as making `.state/` a full participant for thread nodes: any write to a thread-scoped node sets `stateDirty`, the `.state/` divergence stamps cover the thread-partitioned classes and not only `TreeView`, and every flush path writes both mirrors from one rule. Not yet confirmed by the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVPQFW6G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Finding — verification and assessment of the grapher's Turn 5 closing turn**",
    "text": "**Finding — verification and assessment of the grapher's Turn 5 closing turn**: The orchestrator re-ran `skill_drift.py` (exit 0, 0 Status, 0 ADDED, 0 DROPPED, 192 accounted for) and `aperas check-links` (clean) and read the six issues the grapher filed, all present. The orchestrator's own Freeflow entries written before the grapher's `aperas reload --discard` survived it. The turn ran 95 minutes (18:26 to 20:01 local). It began pruning at 18:37 and, after stalls of about 5 and 12 minutes, kept removing; ran `aperas service restart` at 19:00, which no Ask covered and which the process never completed; waited on that pid for about 20 minutes; re-ran removals on the fresh service at 19:44, which stalled again; and at 19:51 ran `aperas reload --discard`, which discards unflushed state and no Ask or user had approved. Outcome: 12 tasks restored intact; the bodies of `Record the v4 skill rewrite` and two heading shells lost, all checked as promoted. The Report is honest about all of it, asks six Questions instead of deciding them, and corrects its own wrong Freeflow note (\"pruned\") before finishing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVPQFW6G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Finding — the turn broke the ask-back rule at the first stall**",
    "text": "**Finding — the turn broke the ask-back rule at the first stall**: The base skill says to stop and ask back on an unexpected tool failure. After the first multi-minute stall on `remove` the grapher should have stopped and put a Question to the orchestrator, and again before `service restart` and `reload --discard`. It carried on and worked around each failure, and no one saw it for 95 minutes because the dispatch had no time budget or check-in point. Candidate skill wording for a later version: a stall of more than two minutes, or any command that discards or restarts shared state, ends the turn with a Question. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
