    "title": "**The ApeironNgn service's resident memory climbs to about 5.8 GB over hours under heavy use, and a fresh service idles at about 1.3 GB for 3.66 MB of data**",
    "text": "**The ApeironNgn service's resident memory climbs to about 5.8 GB over hours under heavy use, and a fresh service idles at about 1.3 GB for 3.66 MB of data**: found 2026-10-02, in the v4.3 skill task, with two parts. First, the old service (pid 220722, up from 16:16) reached 4.74 GB at 19:20 and 5.76 GB at 19:42, a rise of roughly 40 to 50 MB a minute over its last hour, with two threads at 100 percent CPU and almost no disk writes; its V8 heap limit is 2.1 GB, far below its resident size, so most of the memory sat outside the V8 heap. Second, a fresh service idles at 1.05 to 1.5 GB (median 1.34 GB, maximum 2.18 GB) over 379 samples and 63 minutes, while the whole graph on disk is 3.66 MB of JSON-LD, about 37,000 quads, roughly 350 times the data size at rest. The fresh service did not grow over that hour under light use, so the climb to 5 GB needs heavier work (thread-task removals) or a long uptime that the sampler has not seen. Related: the stall in [the thread-task `aperas remove` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000) was recorded at 3.4 to 4.6 GB resident, and the slowdown in [the `npm run verify` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001) and [its open \"why\" entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000) put oxigraph's WASM footprint at 850 MB to 1 GB and ruled it out as the cause of the slowdown, which does not rule it out as a size problem. Not known whether the climb and the stall share a cause. Hypotheses, none tested: the store is Oxigraph's WASM store, whose linear memory grows and never shrinks, and each reload builds a new store, so a leak in reload or removal could keep old stores alive. Measurements still to take: have the service report `process.memoryUsage()` (heap used, external, array buffers) and the store's own size, and start it with `--heapsnapshot-signal=SIGUSR2` so a snapshot can be taken when the process is large, since the external sampler cannot see inside it. Workaround: a restarted service returns to about 1.3 GB, but a restart is for the user to approve. Related, from the investigation's code survey (read, untested): the full-store scan that [the removal-stall issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000) charges per embedded node, `allIdsOfKind`, allocates a JavaScript `Quad` for every store quad on each call (about 40k objects, about 140 ms), which is a candidate source of garbage-collector pressure and of the external-memory growth recorded here; a reload also builds a new `Store` and leaves the old one to the garbage collector, as [the boot and shutdown issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001) describes. The measurements that would tell these apart are listed in [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001). Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
