    "title": "**Finding — the grapher's survey of the slow-service record**",
    "text": "**Finding — the grapher's survey of the slow-service record**: (orchestrator, read from the grapher's Turn 1 Report in `.threads/grapher.md`; a thread node, so not linked) Of eight symptoms, only three have a mechanism established by code reading or reproduction: the migration blocking (a synchronous ingest loop blocks the event loop), the thread-flush routing gap and the tombstone leak. The verify slowdown has two causes ruled out by experiment and its cause open; the removal stall, the memory growth, the slow start and the spurious \"not running\" rest on untested hypotheses. The service has no timing or memory instrumentation of its own and `service.log` holds only startup lines. Block count does not predict the removal stall: removals of 39 and 50 blocks were fast and of 42 and 31 slow. The grapher's own reading, labelled as such: one long synchronous operation on the single-threaded service would explain the removal stall, the spurious \"not running\", the SIGTERM that never completed and the timers that never fire. `design/` has no document for the service lifecycle.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open — the first measurements, in order**",
    "text": "**Open — the first measurements, in order**: (the grapher's list, none of them changes behaviour) 1, event-loop lag and `process.memoryUsage()` logged by the service; 2, per-operation timing split into pre-check, operation, post-check and flush; 3, one controlled `remove` on a fresh service against the same after a long uptime, with the link sweep timed alone; 4, a CPU profile during a stall; 5, a heap snapshot at idle and when large; 6, a timestamped boot log and ping timing; 7, sweep cost against tombstone count; 8, dehydrate time at current node counts, before the thread-flush fix adds work to the 3-second timer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Default — the external sampler moves into the repo as the outside half of measurements 1 to 4, and the design home waits**",
    "text": "**Default — the external sampler moves into the repo as the outside half of measurements 1 to 4, and the design home waits**: (orchestrator, answering the grapher's two questions; not yet confirmed by the user) The sampler is a session scratchpad script that will disappear, so the coder takes it into the repo as investigation tooling and extends it; the in-process half (event-loop lag, memory, per-operation timing, profile, heap snapshot) needs changes in the service. The grapher opens a `design/` document for the service lifecycle once the investigation has settled what the store, reload, sweeps and flush timers do, not before.",
