    "title": "**Every `remove`, `insert`, `update` and `retype` re-parses the whole artifact with remark before and after the operation, about 1.7 to 2.5 ms a block, so a write to a large thread file costs seconds before its own work**",
    "text": "**Every `remove`, `insert`, `update` and `retype` re-parses the whole artifact with remark before and after the operation, about 1.7 to 2.5 ms a block, so a write to a large thread file costs seconds before its own work**: found 2026-10-02 in the slow-service investigation's code survey of the service. Read from `service.ts`: each of the four handlers calls `checkArtifactLinkIntegrity(store, artifactId)` before the operation (`artifactForRef`, then the pre-check, then the `runX` call) and `withLinkCheck` calls it again after, over the whole owning artifact and not only the blocks the operation touched. Each sweep (`collectAllBlockNodes`, then `sweepBlocks`) walks every live block, reads its `text`, parses the whole text with the remark processor (`collectLinkCodesFromText`, no cache), and for each block that has link text reads `.links`, computes `toPath()` and calls `resolveOneCode` per link. Measured in a scratch process: 638 to 932 ms for `.threads/coder.md` (367 live blocks, 65 links), which is about 1.7 to 2.5 ms per live block, so about 1.3 to 1.9 s for the two sweeps; for `.threads/grapher.md` (767 blocks, 487 links) the same rate gives about 1.3 to 1.9 s a sweep, derived and not measured, because the in-memory run on that file did not finish in 100 s. Derived, not measured: the sweeps cost seconds, so on their own they do not reach the minutes of [the removal stall](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000); they are one of three cost centres of a `remove` there. Because the service runs one operation at a time, every second a write spends in the sweeps is also queue wait for every other client, which is the mechanism in [the spurious \"not running\" issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001). Earlier record: [the `npm run verify` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001) names the same kind of operation, `checkLinkIntegrity`'s corpus sweep, as the one that went from low hundreds of milliseconds fresh to 12 to 55 seconds late in a long process; whether the per-write sweep slows the same way in the long-lived service is untested. Fix directions: none decided. Candidates, the grapher's reading and none tried: parse a block's text once and cache the result by that text, so unchanged blocks are not re-parsed; check after the operation only the blocks it touched and the links that point at them; keep the pre-check only where it tells something the post-check cannot. The first measurement is to time the sweep alone on a thread artifact and against block count (item 3 of the investigation's first measurements, which needs per-operation timing the service does not yet have). Related: [the boot and shutdown issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001), whose startup and shutdown sweeps share the full-store scan cost, and [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001), which lists the measurements that would time the sweep. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
