    ],
    "title": "**Boot and shutdown cost grows with the tombstones and sits close to the client's 5 s ready wait and 10 s stop wait**",
    "text": "**Boot and shutdown cost grows with the tombstones and sits close to the client's 5 s ready wait and 10 s stop wait**: found 2026-10-02 in the slow-service investigation's code survey of the service. Boot, read from `service.ts`, runs entirely synchronously before the socket exists: the code fingerprint, the token, `rehydrateStore`, `stampAll` of both mirrors, the startup garbage collection (`tombstoneVacuousContainers`, `pruneUnreachableTombstones`, `pruneStaleUnfolds`, then both flushes if anything changed), the creation of the 10 s and 3 s flush timers and the 30 minute idle timer, then `listen`; only in the listen callback does `markReady` flip the lock to ready, and the startup link sweep runs after that. Measured on a scratch copy: rehydrate about 1.06 s, `tombstoneVacuousContainers` about 1.2 s and `pruneUnreachableTombstones` about 2.3 s even with nothing to prune, the startup link sweep about 0.6 s. Against that, `waitForReady` in `serviceClient.ts` polls `ping()` every 100 ms for 5 s (read): about 1 s of rehydrate and about 3.5 s of startup collection, plus the `tsx` compile, sit close to it, and a service that misses it keeps starting detached while `aperas service start` reports \"did not become ready in time\" and the lock stays `starting` for a 10 s grace (read). Shutdown (read): the SIGTERM handler runs in JavaScript, so it cannot run while an operation is in progress, and its body queues behind everything already waiting; it re-runs the three sweeps and both flushes, which measured about 3.5 s on an idle copy before any per-node hard-delete work, and `stopIfRunning` in `kgService.ts` gives the process only 10 s from the `kill`. That fits \"didn't stop within 10s of SIGTERM\" in [the removal-stall issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000); consistent with it, not shown to be the cause. The cost grows with the tombstones: the next reload, startup or shutdown prunes each unreachable tombstoned block with the same full-store scan as in that issue, about 140 ms a node (measured per scan), so removing a 42-block task adds about 6 s to a later start or stop (derived, 42 times 140 ms, not measured end to end) on top of the fixed 2.3 s and 1.2 s. A reload (any operation carrying `--reload`) runs all of this plus `rehydrateStore()` into a new `Store`, two `stampAll`s and a corpus link sweep in one synchronous run, and the old store is left to the garbage collector while Oxigraph's WASM linear memory never shrinks (read; relevant to [the memory issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQ4Z968001), untested there). A related cost, read and not measured: `unfold`, `fold` and `zoom` set both dirty flags, so each causes a full content dehydrate within 10 s and a state dehydrate within 3 s. Fix directions: none decided. The investigation's first steps are the ones that make the figures visible: one timestamped line per boot phase with its duration, the client logging its poll count and elapsed time when the wait fails, and sweep cost measured against tombstone count and dehydrate time at current node counts (measurements 6, 7 and 8 of the first measurements). Whether the sweeps can run after the socket exists, or the waits can grow with the work, is a design question not yet raised; `design/` has no document for the service lifecycle. Related: [the spurious \"not running\" issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001), because a boot of 4 to 5 s against the client's 5 s wait is one way a start reads as failed; [the whole-artifact re-parse issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001); and [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001), which lists the timestamped boot log. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
