    "title": "**The service has no timing, memory or load-test coverage, and its one durable log holds only startup lines**",
    "text": "**The service has no timing, memory or load-test coverage, and its one durable log holds only startup lines**: found 2026-10-02 in the slow-service investigation's code survey of the service. In the service (read): there is no timing or memory logging; `APERAS_DEBUG_TIMING` output is lost because `spawnService` starts it with `stdio: 'ignore'`; `logService` (`service.log`) is the only durable channel and holds startup lines only, so a stall of 5.4 or 12 minutes left no line. In the tests and scripts (read): `verify.ts` (749 lines) and `verifyExtended.ts` (757 lines) run in-process against a `rehydrateStore()` copy, never import `service.ts` or `serviceClient.ts`, never spawn the service, and never call `runRemove` or `withLinkCheck`; `verifySupport.ts#instrumentStepTiming`, a `console.log` wrapper that times each \"N. Testing...\" step, is the only timing harness and produced the record in [the `npm run verify` entry](#id/BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001); `apeironNgnSmokeTest.ts` checks rehydrate and field access only; `packages/web/devApiServer.ts` is one more concurrent client that calls `request()` with no timeout but is not a load test. There are no concurrent-client, latency or long-uptime scripts, and no test of the timers, `reloadStore`, `shutdown` or the SIGTERM path. The external sampler, a Python script in a session scratch area outside the repo, has these gaps (read from the script and its CSV): the lock path (`/run/user/1000/aperas/apeironngn.lock`), the probe node (`BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0000`, which a future prune or another graph can remove, turning every probe into a false failure) and `aperas` on `PATH` are hard-coded; the probe is a full `aperas show` client, so each probe time includes a `tsx` client start (541 ms on the first row) and a 60 s cadence cannot resolve a stall of seconds; `probe()` keeps only whether `\"@id\"` appears in stdout and discards stderr and the exit code, so the 300 ms ping failure, a request error and the 20 s timeout all read as a failed probe, which is exactly what the 11 failed probes need to be told apart by; it has no raw ping over the unix socket at 1 s cadence, no separate main-thread and garbage-collector-thread columns, no `VmHWM`, no `/proc/<pid>/io` write bytes, no mirror-file size or modification time (flush cadence would be visible for free), only a client count and not the verbs of the running `aperas` commands, no join with `service.log` by time, no stall durations or memory slope in its report, no metadata row, and no test. Fix directions, from the investigation's Freeflow, the orchestrator's recommendations and none decided: instrumentation in a new `apeironNgn/serviceDiagnostics.ts` behind one environment switch read by `spawnService` (which already copies `process.env`): an event-loop delay histogram, `process.memoryUsage()` and heap statistics every 30 to 60 s, one timestamped line per boot phase, and per-operation timings split into queue wait, pre-check, operation, flush and post-check for operations over about 250 ms, all through `logService`. Limits (read, unverified where marked): the histogram records only when the loop turns, so a 5-minute stall appears as one sample after it ends, and a worker-thread watchdog on a `SharedArrayBuffer` heartbeat would see it live (not built or tried); `--heapsnapshot-signal=SIGUSR2` can reach the service through `NODE_OPTIONS` with no code change but blocks the loop on a large process, needs about that much memory again, and misses the WASM memory (the V8 heap is about 2.1 GB against up to 5.8 GB resident); `--cpu-prof` writes only at exit and cannot capture a stuck or killed process; for a live stall an inspector or `perf` is the candidate (unverified). The sampler moves into the repo as Python under `monorepo/packages/cli/diagnostics/` with a `svc:sample` script, adds a raw-socket ping each second that keeps the failure text, and drops the one-minute client probe. The first measurements, in order: event-loop lag and `process.memoryUsage()` logged by the service; per-operation timing; one controlled `remove` on a fresh service against the same after a long uptime, with the link sweep timed alone; a CPU profile during a stall; a heap snapshot at idle and when large; a timestamped boot log and ping timing; sweep cost against tombstone count; dehydrate time at current node counts. This is the missing ground for [the spurious \"not running\" issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001), [the removal stall](#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000) and [the memory issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQ4Z968001), whose mechanisms rest on code reading and one reproduction each. Related: [the whole-artifact re-parse issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001) and [the boot and shutdown issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001), the two cost centres other than the removal scan whose figures are measured only in a scratch process. Filed 2026-10-02.",
