    "title": "**A cross-reference is hand-assembled Markdown, because the canonical address cannot be authored and the conversion at ingest and at projection is designed and not built**",
    "text": "**A cross-reference is hand-assembled Markdown, because the canonical address cannot be authored and the conversion at ingest and at projection is designed and not built**: found 2026-10-02 in the skill wording task, when the orchestrator cited bare ids and the design owner pointed to the intended flow; it has waited weeks. The design (read): inside the graph a reference is a canonical address, [the writer addresses a target by `id/<ID>` or `aperas://id/<ID>` after ingestion](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG009), [the canonical syntax uses the `aperas://` scheme](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8009) and is resolved against the graph; in a projected artifact the same reference is the MD-compatible link, relative path and anchor, generated from the target's current location. The conversion belongs at the two boundaries, compat to canon at ingest and canon to compat at projection, so the writer never types a path or an anchor ([the linking design](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T0001)). The record (read): [the history of the linking work](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ7698002) notes that the target design replaces the older `[[code]]` wrapper with that syntax. Neither direction is built: a stored node still holds the compat form (`[title](../design/x.md#id/BlockNode:…)`), which is why the Report and Ask templates in the skills teach a relative-path link, so the writer works out the artifact path and the anchor form by hand for every reference, and why a renamed or moved artifact breaks every compat link that points at it. The friction showed up as the orchestrator citing bare ids, which create no link. Observation, offline read of `collectLinkCodesFromText` in `astParser.ts`, nothing written to the graph: `[label](aperas://id/BlockNode:X)`, `[label](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:X)` and `[label](#id/BlockNode:X)` are each taken as a link; `[[BlockNode:X]]`, a bare `aperas://id/BlockNode:X` and a code-spanned `BlockNode:X` are not, so the form `tree` and `unfold` print creates no link when pasted. Untested: whether an `aperas://id/` link persists through `insert`, what the projection writes for it, and whether `check-links` accepts it. Fix direction (the design owner's, stated and not yet implemented): accept a canonical address wherever a reference is expected in written text, bare or with an optional label; have ingest store the canonical form; have projection generate the compat link (relative path, anchor, and a label taken from the target's current title). Its place in the tools queue is the design owner's call. Related: [`check-links` and the write-time summary treat an id link to a nonexistent node as resolved](#id/BlockNode:00CVKFWNB8001), which concerns the same `aperas://id/` branch. Filed 2026-10-02.",
