    "title": "**Finding — measured: a removal's cost is garbage-collection time driven by external memory, not the scan count alone**",
    "text": "**Finding — measured: a removal's cost is garbage-collection time driven by external memory, not the scan count alone**: (orchestrator, from the coder's Turn 2 Report in `.threads/coder.md`, a thread node and so not linked; seven bounded runs on `/tmp` copies of the store, nothing written to the real graph or the live service) Removing one `#### Turn` (34 embedded nodes) cost about 0.6 s in the pre-sweep, 9 to 10.6 s in `runRemove`, 19 to 29 s in the content flush and 0.2 s in the post-sweep, so 30 to 40 s with the loop blocked, and the state flush adds 16 to 20 s. `runRemove` did exactly 34 full-store scans, one per embedded node, at 208 to 352 ms each. The heaviest task (122 embedded nodes) was still inside `runRemove` when the 60-second limit hit, after 60 scans, and the average cost per scan rose from 130 ms to 3,013 ms across them. Under `--trace-gc`, Mark-Compact time per ten scans rose from 1.0 s to 28.3 s of that window's 29.2 s, triggered by external memory pressure: each scan retains about 9 MB of heap and 21 MB of external memory until the event loop turns, and resident size reached 2.0 GB after 60 scans. Repeated scans alone slow down; 160 deletions did not, and a fresh `Store` did not restore the speed. Content and state flushes cost 3 to 4 times as much after a removal. A task of about 120 embedded nodes therefore plausibly takes minutes, which fits [the recorded removal stall](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CVNX6A3R000) of 5.4 and 12 minutes, and the same retained external memory fits [the memory growth](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CVQ4Z968001); neither is proven, because no run finished a removal of that size and `.threads/grapher.md` was not measured. The finalizer explanation for the retained memory is a reading of the numbers, since the Oxigraph source was not read.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQY28QR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Finding — the sampler is in the repo and works**",
    "text": "**Finding — the sampler is in the repo and works**: (orchestrator, from the same Report) `monorepo/packages/cli/diagnostics/svc_sampler.py`, with 11 tests and `svc:sample` and `svc:sample:test` scripts in `monorepo/package.json`, staged and not committed. It pings the unix socket once a second and keeps the failure text, records `VmHWM`, `/proc/<pid>/io` deltas and mirror-file sizes and mtimes, takes the lock path and probe node as options, and its `--report` adds stall durations, a memory slope, a failure-text histogram and flush counts. The orchestrator ran `npm run svc:sample:test`: 11 tests, OK. A 61-second live run against the service had 61 of 61 pings answered in 0.3 to 3.5 ms. The orchestrator's own scratchpad sampler ran 740 samples over two hours with 16 failed probes; it is stopped, its CSV is kept in the scratchpad only.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQY28QR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open — three choices for the user**",
    "text": "**Open — three choices for the user**: (the coder's Questions, with its recommendations) 1, the fix direction for the removal stall: change `removeDanglingUnfolds` (`node.ts:251`) to one object-bound `store.match(null, unfolds, id, null)` in place of a view scan and a match per view, alone and first, verified on a scratch copy and then a separate approval for the repo edit; larger options are one pass per removal instead of one per node, fewer full scans in `dehydrateToJsonLd` and the boot sweeps, and yielding to the loop between large operations. 2, whether the written design for `serviceDiagnostics.ts` stands, with four points: a worker-thread watchdog left out of the first cut, the slow-operation line emitted from a `setImmediate` to capture garbage-collection time (unverified for a long synchronous run), the event-loop-delay line written only above 100 ms or every sixth window, and `NODE_OPTIONS` in the spawn environment rather than argv. 3, the old sampler process, now stopped.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVRB63Z0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CVRB63Z0000:props:StringProp:00CVRB6D78000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CVRB63Z0000:props:StringProp:00CVRB6D78001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Finding — a subagent can message the orchestrator mid-flight, and both directions deliver at tool-call boundaries**",
    "text": "**Finding — a subagent can message the orchestrator mid-flight, and both directions deliver at tool-call boundaries**: (user: \"Now we do some small test to see if a subagent can message back mid-flight.\") The orchestrator started a dummy `general-purpose` subagent on five 25-second sleeps and sent it a token message at 22:32:36. The message reached it at 22:32:50, as a queued command right after the tool call it was inside returned (it had moved its first sleep to a background command after the harness blocked the foreground `sleep 25`). Two seconds later the subagent called `SendMessage` with `to: \"main\"`; the tool answered that the message was queued for the main conversation's next turn. The reply reached the orchestrator at about 22:32:59, between two tool calls, while the subagent was still on its remaining steps and well before its final report. The round trip was about 23 seconds, nearly all of it waiting for tool-call boundaries on each side. `SendMessage` was a deferred tool for the subagent and had to be loaded with `ToolSearch`. The earlier survey found no subagent using it unprompted, and the `SubagentHandback` description says it is not a messaging channel, so the skills still treat the turn's end as the only reliable way for a subagent to ask. Origin and history (the user's reading: \"it's clear why the one-shot handoff impression is strong: it was true until v2.1.77. Only very recently, was the SendMessage -- originally designed for Agent Teams -- retargeted to subagent!\"): mid-flight messaging is the tool's first design, not an upgrade. `SendMessage` began as the inter-agent tool of Agent Teams, an experimental toolset behind `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS`, where teammates run at the same time and message each other through per-agent mailbox files, delivered automatically (Claude Code docs, \"Orchestrate teams of Claude Code sessions\"; an early-February 2026 sighting of the toolset is from a search snippet only). In v2.1.77 (an issue of 2026-03-17 reports it) the Agent tool lost its `resume` parameter and `SendMessage` with the agent's id or name became how a finished subagent is continued, which is when it was retargeted to subagents, at first still gated behind the Agent Teams flag; the docs now also say subagents that Claude named can message each other. In v2.1.224 (2026-08-07) it gained delivery between separate sessions, with `ListAgents`. What the orchestrator did not verify: that before v2.1.77 no running subagent could receive a message, which is the user's inference from the removal of `resume`, and the exact release that first shipped `SendMessage` in Agent Teams.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
